\par
\{ \par
    id: 3, \par
    title: "Lesson 3: The Lordship of Jesus Christ", \par
    description: "Understanding the authority of Christ over every area of the believer's life.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Many accept Jesus as Savior.<br />\par
        Fewer submit to Him as Lord.<br />\par
        Salvation deals with forgiveness.<br />\par
        Lordship deals with authority.<br />\par
        You cannot separate what Scripture joins. The same Jesus who saves is the Jesus who rules.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. What Does \ldblquote Lord\rdblquote  Mean?</p>\par
        <p>Key Text: Romans 10:9<br />\par
        \ldblquote If you confess with your mouth the Lord Jesus and believe in your heart that God has raised Him from the dead, you will be saved.\rdblquote </p>\par
        <p>The word Lord (Greek: <em>Kyrios</em>) means:</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Master</li>\par
          <li>Owner</li>\par
          <li>Sovereign ruler</li>\par
          <li>One with absolute authority</li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p>In the Roman world, calling someone \ldblquote Lord\rdblquote  implied allegiance and submission.<br />\par
        In the Jewish context, \ldblquote Lord\rdblquote  was used for Yahweh.</p>\par
        <p>To call Jesus \ldblquote Lord\rdblquote  is to declare:</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>He has authority over my life.</li>\par
          <li>I belong to Him.</li>\par
          <li>My will submits to His will.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. The Biblical Foundation of Christ\rquote s Lordship</p>\par
        <p className="font-semibold">A. His Lordship by Identity</p>\par
        <p>Jesus is Lord because of who He is.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>John 1:1\endash 3 \endash  Creator</li>\par
          <li>Colossians 1:15\endash 18 \endash  Head of the Church</li>\par
          <li>Philippians 2:9\endash 11 \endash  Every knee shall bow</li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p>Christ is not voted into Lordship. He is inherently Lord.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-1">B. His Lordship by Redemption</p>\par
        <p>1 Corinthians 6:19\endash 20<br />\par
        \ldblquote\f0 You are not your own\f1\'85 \f0 you were bought at a price.\f1\rdblquote </p>\par
        <p>Salvation is not self-improvement. It is transfer of ownership.<br />\par
        Before Christ \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 enslaved to sin (Romans 6:17).<br />\par
        After Christ \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 servants of righteousness (Romans 6:18).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. Lordship vs Mere Profession</p>\par
        <p>Matthew 7:21<br />\par
\f1         \ldblquote\f0 Not everyone who says to Me, \f1\lquote\f0 Lord, Lord,\f1\rquote  \f0 shall enter the kingdom\f1\'85\rdblquote\f0 </p>\par
        <p>There is: Verbal lordship and Practical lordship.<br />\par
        A person may confess Christ publicly yet resist His authority privately.</p>\par
        <p>True lordship affects: Decisions, Relationships, Finances, Ambitions, Morality.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">4. Dimensions of Christ\f1\rquote\f0 s Lordship</p>\par
        <p><strong>A. Lordship Over the Mind (Romans 12:2):</strong> Renewed thinking, Biblical worldview, Submission of opinions to Scripture. A disciple does not argue with Scripture \f1\emdash  he aligns with it.</p>\par
        <p><strong>B. Lordship Over the Will (Luke 22:42):</strong> \ldblquote Not My will, but Yours be done.\rdblquote  Lordship means surrendering: Personal plans, Ego, Preferences.</p>\par
        <p><strong>C. Lordship Over the Body (1 Corinthians 6:18\endash 20):</strong> Sexual purity, Self-control, Physical discipline. Your body belongs to Christ.</p>\par
        <p><strong>D. Lordship Over Possessions (Luke 16:10\endash 11):</strong> Stewardship, Giving, Integrity. If Christ is not Lord of your wallet, He is not fully Lord of your life.</p>\par
        <p><strong>E. Lordship Over Relationships (Colossians 3:18\endash 24):</strong> Marriage, Parenting, Workplace conduct. Lordship transforms social behavior.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">5. The Cost of Rejecting His Lordship</p>\par
        <p>When believers resist Christ\rquote s authority:</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Spiritual stagnation</li>\par
          <li>Loss of intimacy</li>\par
          <li>Discipline from God (Hebrews 12:6)</li>\par
          <li>Ineffectiveness</li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p>Partial obedience is still disobedience.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">6. The Blessings of Submitting to His Lordship</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Peace (Isaiah 26:3)</li>\par
          <li>Direction (Proverbs 3:5\endash 6)</li>\par
          <li>Fruitfulness (John 15:5)</li>\par
          <li>Eternal reward (2 Timothy 4:7\endash 8)</li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p>Lordship is not bondage \emdash  it is alignment with divine design.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">7. Biblical Examples</p>\par
        <p><strong>A. The Rich Young Ruler (Mark 10:17\endash 22):</strong> He wanted eternal life but refused surrender. His wealth ruled him more than Christ.</p>\par
        <p><strong>B. Paul the Apostle (Acts 9:6):</strong> \ldblquote Lord, what do You want me to do?\rdblquote  Conversion immediately produced surrender.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">8. Cultural Reflection</p>\par
        <p>In many contexts: Jesus is consulted but not obeyed. Prayer replaces submission. Spiritual gifts replace character.<br />\par
        But the gospel demands total allegiance.</p>\par
        <p>Christianity without lordship becomes: Emotional religion, Cultural identity, Performance spirituality.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">9. Practical Application</p>\par
        <p className="font-semibold">A. Lordship Assessment Questions</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Are there areas I have not surrendered?</li>\par
          <li>Do I obey only when convenient?</li>\par
          <li>Does Scripture overrule my opinions?</li>\par
          <li>Can I say \ldblquote no\rdblquote  to sin quickly?</li>\par
          <li>Is Christ truly directing my future?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-1">B. Daily Lordship Practice</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Begin each day with surrender prayer.</li>\par
          <li>Make decisions through Scripture.</li>\par
          <li>Practice immediate obedience.</li>\par
          <li>Invite accountability.</li>\par
          <li>Regularly evaluate your motives.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-1">C. Personal Lordship Declaration</p>\par
        <p>\ldblquote I acknowledge Jesus Christ as Lord over: My thoughts, My ambitions, My finances, My relationships, My future. I surrender my will and choose obedience daily.\rdblquote </p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Why can Jesus not be Savior without being Lord?</li>\par
          <li>What is the difference between belief and surrender?</li>\par
          <li>Which area of your life is hardest to submit?</li>\par
          <li>How does lordship affect daily decisions?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PERSONAL REFLECTION</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Am I negotiating with God in certain areas?</li>\par
          <li>Where am I resisting His authority?</li>\par
          <li>What would full surrender look like practically?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Memorize Romans 10:9.</li>\par
          <li>Write down every area of your life and consciously surrender them in prayer.</li>\par
          <li>Identify one area of delayed obedience and act on it this week.</li>\par
          <li>Share your commitment with your accountability partner.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">MEMORY VERSE</p>\par
        <p>Philippians 2:11 \emdash  \ldblquote And that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father.\rdblquote </p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
