\{ \par
    id: 4, \par
    title: "Lesson 4: Repentance, Faith & Identity in Christ", \par
    description: "Turning from sin, trusting Christ, and embracing your new nature.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">PART ONE: REPENTANCE & FAITH</p>\par
        <p>Salvation is received through repentance toward God and faith in our Lord Jesus Christ (Acts 20:21). These are not two separate roads \emdash  they are two sides of the same response to the gospel.</p>\par
        <p>Repentance turns us from sin.<br />\par
        Faith turns us toward Christ.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. What is Repentance?</p>\par
        <p className="font-semibold">Biblical Meaning</p>\par
        <p>The Greek word <em>metanoia</em> means: Change of mind, Change of heart, Change of direction. It is not merely feeling sorry. It is a decisive turning.</p>\par
        <p>Key Texts: Acts 3:19, 2 Corinthians 7:10, Luke 13:3</p>\par
        <p>Repentance involves: Recognition of sin, Genuine sorrow, Confession, Turning away, A new direction of obedience.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. True Repentance vs Emotional Regret</p>\par
        <p>2 Corinthians 7:10 \emdash  \ldblquote\f0 Godly sorrow produces repentance leading to salvation\f1\'85 \f0 but the sorrow of the world produces death.\f1\rdblquote </p>\par
        <p><strong>Emotional Regret:</strong> Feels bad about consequences, Cries but does not change, Is concerned about reputation, Returns to the same behavior. Example: Judas (Matthew 27:3\endash 5).</p>\par
        <p><strong>True Repentance:</strong> Feels grief over offending God, Confesses sincerely, Makes restitution where possible, Demonstrates lasting change. Example: Peter (John 21:15\endash 19).</p>\par
        <p>Repentance is proven over time by fruit (Luke 3:8).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. Faith: Trusting Christ Completely</p>\par
        <p>Faith is: Personal reliance on Christ, Trust in His finished work, Surrender to His authority.</p>\par
        <p>Key Texts: Ephesians 2:8\endash 9, Hebrews 11:1, Romans 5:1</p>\par
        <p>Saving faith is not: Mental agreement, Cultural Christianity, Crisis prayer only. It is active trust that produces obedience (James 2:17).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">4. Counting the Cost</p>\par
        <p>Luke 14:25\endash 33 \emdash  Jesus warned that following Him requires sober evaluation.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>A. Supreme Loyalty (v. 26):</strong> Christ above family, reputation, and comfort.</li>\par
          <li><strong>B. Bearing the Cross (v. 27):</strong> Willingness to suffer loss.</li>\par
          <li><strong>C. Calculated Commitment (v. 28\endash 30):</strong> Like building a tower \emdash  consider what it will demand.</li>\par
          <li><strong>D. Total Surrender (v. 33):</strong> \ldblquote Whoever of you does not forsake all that he has cannot be My disciple.\rdblquote </li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p>Christianity is not casual attachment. It is total allegiance.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">5. Public Confession & Baptism</p>\par
        <p><strong>A. Public Confession (Romans 10:9\endash 10):</strong> Faith is inward belief expressed outwardly. True salvation does not remain secret indefinitely. We confess Christ publicly.</p>\par
        <p><strong>B. Baptism (Matthew 28:19, Acts 2:38, Romans 6:3\endash 4):</strong> Baptism represents: Death to old life, Burial of the old self, Resurrection into new life. It does not save \emdash  but it publicly identifies us with Christ. It is: An act of obedience, A declaration of allegiance, A visible sign of inward transformation.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART TWO: IDENTITY IN CHRIST</p>\par
        <p>Repentance changes direction. Faith changes position. Identity changes perception. Many believers are saved but still live as if condemned.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. Who You Were vs Who You Are</p>\par
        <p><strong>Who You Were:</strong> Dead in sin (Ephesians 2:1), Slave to sin (Romans 6:17), Alienated from God (Colossians 1:21), Under condemnation (Romans 5:18).</p>\par
        <p><strong>Who You Are:</strong> Alive in Christ (Ephesians 2:5), Righteous in Him (2 Corinthians 5:21), Child of God (John 1:12), Chosen generation (1 Peter 2:9).</p>\par
        <p>Identity transformation is foundational for spiritual growth.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. New Creation Reality</p>\par
        <p>2 Corinthians 5:17 \emdash  \ldblquote\f0 If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation\f1\'85\rdblquote </p>\par
        <p>This means: New spiritual nature, New desires, New allegiance, New destiny. The old identity no longer defines you. Past failures are history \emdash  not identity.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. Freedom from Condemnation</p>\par
        <p>Romans 8:1 \emdash  \ldblquote\f0 There is therefore now no condemnation\f1\'85\rdblquote </p>\par
        <p>Conviction leads to repentance. Condemnation leads to paralysis. The believer may struggle or stumble, but is no longer under divine judgment. Guilt has been paid for at the cross.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">4. Authority of the Believer</p>\par
        <p>Salvation is not weakness \emdash  it is empowerment (Luke 10:19, Ephesians 1:19\endash 23, Colossians 2:15). Because we are in Christ: We have authority over sin, We resist the devil (James 4:7), We stand firm in truth. Authority flows from position \emdash  not from emotion.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL APPLICATION</p>\par
        <p><strong>A. Repentance Check:</strong> Is there any habitual sin I am tolerating? Have I truly turned from it? Have I made restitution where necessary?</p>\par
        <p><strong>B. Identity Affirmation:</strong> Write down: \ldblquote I am in Christ: Forgiven, Accepted, Righteous, Empowered, Adopted, Commissioned.\rdblquote  Repeat Scripture-based truths daily.</p>\par
        <p><strong>C. Living in Authority:</strong> Reject condemning thoughts. Speak Scripture over your life. Practice obedience immediately. Resist temptation confidently. Walk in accountability.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>What is the difference between regret and repentance?</li>\par
          <li>Why did Jesus emphasize counting the cost?</li>\par
          <li>Why must faith be public?</li>\par
          <li>How does identity affect behavior?</li>\par
          <li>How can believers walk in authority without pride?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PERSONAL REFLECTION</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Have I truly repented or merely felt bad?</li>\par
          <li>Is there an area I have not surrendered?</li>\par
          <li>Am I living as a new creation or rehearsing my past?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Memorize Romans 8:1.</li>\par
          <li>Publicly share your testimony if you haven\rquote t.</li>\par
          <li>Identify one old identity label you must reject.</li>\par
          <li>Write a declaration of who you are in Christ.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">MEMORY VERSE</p>\par
        <p>2 Corinthians 5:17 \emdash  \ldblquote Therefore, if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation; old things have passed away; behold, all things have become new.\rdblquote </p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
