\{ \par
    id: 10, \par
    title: "Lesson 10: Renewing the Mind & Integrity", \par
    description: "Breaking mental strongholds, healing emotional wounds, and establishing moral purity.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Discipleship without transformation of the mind is cosmetic. A person can appear religious yet be spiritually defeated internally. True discipleship starts in the mind: <em>\ldblquote\f0 Do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind\f1\'85\rdblquote </em> (Romans 12:2).</p>\par
        <p>Unrenewed thoughts produce fear, unhealthy habits, and compromise. This lesson forms the inner foundation of lasting discipleship.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: RENEWING THE MIND</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. Breaking Strongholds</p>\par
        <p>Strongholds are patterns of thought that resist God\rquote s truth (2 Corinthians 10:4\endash 5). Examples include fear of failure, unworthiness, shame, or resentment.</p>\par
        <p><strong>Steps to Victory:</strong> Identify the lie, bring it to Scripture, declare truth aloud, and maintain consistent replacement through repetition and accountability.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. Replacing Lies With Truth</p>\par
        <p>The mind is like soil; what is planted will grow. Lies produce defeat, but truth produces freedom.</p>\par
        <table className="min-w-full border-collapse border border-gray-300 my-2">\par
          <thead>\par
            <tr className="bg-gray-100">\par
              <th className="border border-gray-300 p-2 text-left">The Lie</th>\par
              <th className="border border-gray-300 p-2 text-left">The Truth (Scripture)</th>\par
            </tr>\par
          </thead>\par
          <tbody>\par
            <tr>\par
              <td className="border border-gray-300 p-2">I am worthless</td>\par
              <td className="border border-gray-300 p-2">\ldblquote I am fearfully and wonderfully made\rdblquote  (Ps 139:14)</td>\par
            </tr>\par
            <tr>\par
              <td className="border border-gray-300 p-2">I will always fail</td>\par
              <td className="border border-gray-300 p-2">\ldblquote I can do all things through Christ...\rdblquote  (Phil 4:13)</td>\par
            </tr>\par
            <tr>\par
              <td className="border border-gray-300 p-2">I am alone</td>\par
              <td className="border border-gray-300 p-2">\ldblquote I will never leave you...\rdblquote  (Heb 13:5)</td>\par
            </tr>\par
            <tr>\par
              <td className="border border-gray-300 p-2">God cannot use me</td>\par
              <td className="border border-gray-300 p-2">\ldblquote My grace is sufficient for you\rdblquote  (2 Cor 12:9)</td>\par
            </tr>\par
          </tbody>\par
        </table>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. Emotional Healing & Freedom from Past Wounds</p>\par
        <p>Unhealed emotions hinder growth. Healing is gradual but intentional. Stop identifying with past mistakes; accept God\rquote s forgiveness fully (1 John 1:9). You are a new creation (2 Corinthians 5:17).</p>\par
        <p><strong>Practical Steps:</strong> Acknowledge the wound, forgive offenders (Matthew 6:14\endash 15), seek godly counsel, and replace pain with purpose.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: INTEGRITY & MORAL PURITY</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. Sexual & Financial Integrity</p>\par
        <p><strong>Sexual Purity:</strong> Protects your mind and destiny (1 Thessalonians 4:3\endash 5). Guard your media consumption and maintain accountability.</p>\par
        <p><strong>Financial Integrity:</strong> Kingdom security (Proverbs 11:1). Avoid dishonesty in business, pay debts promptly, and be transparent with resources.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. Truthfulness & Guarding the Heart</p>\par
        <p>Truthfulness sustains credibility. God honors honesty over cleverness. <strong>Proverbs 4:23</strong> commands us to guard our hearts above all else because it determines the course of our lives.</p>\par
        <p><strong>Filter your Command Center:</strong> Thoughts \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Choices \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Actions \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Destiny.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL 30-DAY PLAN</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Week 1 (Mind):</strong> Memorize one truth daily; journal and replace lies.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 2 (Healing):</strong> Identify one past wound and take a step to forgive/release it.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 3 (Purity):</strong> Audit habits; make one actionable change in boundaries or budgeting.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 4 (Guarding):</strong> Reflect on influences; remove one toxic habit or relationship.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Which lies have been most persistent in your life?</li>\par
          <li>How do past wounds affect your obedience and faith?</li>\par
          <li>What are practical ways to guard your heart daily?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Begin daily Scripture meditation on your identity in Christ.</li>\par
          <li>Write down three specific lies you\f1\rquote\f0 ve believed and the verses that debunk them.</li>\par
          <li>Take one practical step toward financial or moral integrity this week.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>Lies in the mind lead to defeat; truth leads to freedom. Discipleship is not surface deep\f1\emdash it is mind-deep, heart-deep, and life-deep.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 11, \par
    title: "Lesson 11: Kingdom Living & Practical Christianity", \par
    description: "Applying biblical ethics, decision-making, and stewardship to every area of daily life.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Discipleship is not proven in church services; it is proven in daily life. Kingdom living means Christ governs your decisions, Scripture shapes your worldview, and integrity defines your lifestyle. Jesus called us to live His teachings, not just admire them (Matthew 7:24).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: CHRISTIAN ETHICS</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. A Biblical Worldview</p>\par
        <p>A worldview is the lens through which you interpret reality. A disciple must think biblically before acting practically, rooted in Creation, the Fall, Redemption, and Restoration. Without it, culture and feelings become your compass.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. Christian Decision Making</p>\par
        <p>Seek principles instead of feelings. Use these filters (1 Corinthians 10:23, 31):</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Is it Scripturally permissible?</li>\par
          <li>Does it glorify God?</li>\par
          <li>Does it build others up?</li>\par
          <li>Will it strengthen my character?</li>\par
          <li>Can I do it with a clear conscience?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. Social Media Conduct</p>\par
        <p>Your digital footprint reveals your maturity. Reflect Christ online by avoiding gossip, anger, and mockery (Ephesians 4:29). Before posting, ask: <em>Does this glorify Christ? Would I say this in person?</em></p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">4. Handling Offense</p>\par
        <p>Offense is inevitable (Luke 17:1), but it destroys ministries when pride goes unexamined. <strong>The Biblical Response:</strong> Examine yourself, address the person privately (Matthew 18:15), and forgive quickly (Ephesians 4:32).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: STEWARDSHIP</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. Time Management</p>\par
        <p>Time is life measured. Redeem the time (Ephesians 5:16) by scheduling devotion first and practicing Sabbath rest. Unplanned time often becomes wasted time.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. Financial Stewardship</p>\par
        <p>Money reflects trust. Honor the Lord with your possessions (Proverbs 3:9). Practically: budget, save, give consistently, and avoid debt driven by comparison.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. Talent & Gift Stewardship</p>\par
        <p>God expects development, not neglect (Parable of the Talents, Matthew 25). Improve your skills through study, practice, and mentorship for Kingdom impact.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">4. Accountability Structures</p>\par
        <p>Independence breeds compromise. Protection comes through spiritual mentors, peer partners, and local church commitment (James 5:16). Accountability protects your destiny.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL 30-DAY PLAN</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> Audit social media and decision-making filters.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> Address one unresolved offense or initiate reconciliation.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> Build/revise your financial budget and weekly schedule.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Identify one neglected gift and find an accountability partner.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Does my lifestyle reflect kingdom values or cultural trends?</li>\par
          <li>Am I reactive or principled in decision making?</li>\par
          <li>How can I improve my digital witness this week?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Review your spending for the last month against Kingdom priorities.</li>\par
          <li>Reach out to one person you have been holding an offense against.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize 1 Corinthians 4:2.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>Kingdom living is not mystical\emdash it is practical. Christianity is not only believed; it is demonstrated in how you speak, spend, and forgive.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 12, \par
    title: "Lesson 12: Relationships & Community", \par
    description: "Mastering the arts of forgiveness, reconciliation, and biblical authority within the body of Christ.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>No disciple grows in isolation. Christianity is personal\emdash but never private. Salvation brings you into a family, not just a belief system. God declared: <em>\ldblquote It is not good that man should be alone\rdblquote </em> (Genesis 2:18).</p>\par
        <p>Spiritual maturity is revealed in how we forgive, handle conflict, and maintain unity. You cannot claim Christlikeness while being relationally dysfunctional. Destiny flows through relationships.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: FORGIVENESS</p>\par
        <p>Forgiveness is releasing someone from the personal debt they owe you. It is a decision before it is a feeling (Matthew 6:14\endash 15). It is <strong>NOT</strong> pretending the hurt didn't happen or automatically restoring trust. Trust is rebuilt over time, but forgiveness releases the bitterness that causes spiritual stagnation (Hebrews 12:15).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: RECONCILIATION</p>\par
        <p>Forgiveness can be one-sided, but reconciliation requires mutual participation: <strong>Repentance, Ownership of wrong, and Willingness to restore trust.</strong> We are called as ministers of reconciliation (2 Corinthians 5:18). If reconciliation isn't immediate, forgiveness must still occur.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: BIBLICAL CONFLICT RESOLUTION</p>\par
        <p>Conflict is inevitable; division is optional. Jesus gave a clear process in <strong>Matthew 18:15\endash 17</strong>:</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Step One: Go Privately.</strong> Speak directly. No gossip, no alliances, no posting online.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Step Two: Bring Witnesses.</strong> Involve mature believers for clarity and restoration.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Step Three: Church Leadership.</strong> Intervene for order and unity.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p>Be quick to listen and slow to anger (James 1:19). Ask: <em>Am I seeking resolution or revenge?</em></p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART IV: SUBMISSION TO AUTHORITY</p>\par
        <p>Submission is divine order, not oppression. All authority ultimately comes from God (Romans 13:1). It is voluntary alignment and respect, even when disagreeing. It does <strong>NOT</strong> mean enabling abuse or endorsing sin (Acts 5:29). Submission cultivates humility and spiritual protection.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">BUILDING HEALTHY COMMUNITY</p>\par
        <p>Healthy community requires clear communication, mutual honor, and shared accountability (Ephesians 4:2\endash 3). Avoid these pitfalls: Passive aggression, unspoken expectations, and gossip disguised as "concern."</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL 30-DAY PLAN</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> Identify an unresolved offense and begin the forgiveness process.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> Initiate one needed reconciliation conversation.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> Apply Matthew 18 steps to an active conflict.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Pray intentionally for your leaders (government, church, work).</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Do I speak <em>about</em> people more than I speak <em>to</em> them?</li>\par
          <li>How do I distinguish between forgiveness and the restoration of trust?</li>\par
          <li>Is my heart submitted or resistant to authority?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Reach out to one person to bless them or resolve a minor tension.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Ephesians 4:32.</li>\par
          <li>Write down one leader you struggle with and pray for them daily.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>The world recognizes Christ by our love (John 13:35). If relationships are fractured, discipleship collapses. Forgive quickly, reconcile intentionally, and resolve conflict biblically.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
