\par
\{ \par
    id: 13, \par
    title: "Lesson 13: Marriage & Family", \par
    description: "Understanding God's divine design for marriage, the principles of parenting, and the purpose of singleness.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Marriage and family are divine institutions, created by God before government or the church. Strong families build strong nations, while broken ones create generational wounds. This lesson is for everyone\emdash singles, engaged, married, and parents alike.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: GOD\rquote S DESIGN FOR MARRIAGE</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. Origin and Purpose</p>\par
        <p>Marriage is a <strong>covenant</strong>, not a contract; a union joined by God (Matthew 19:4\endash 6). It exists for: \par
          <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
            <li>Companionship (Partnership)</li>\par
            <li>Covenant Reflection (Reflecting Christ and the Church, Ephesians 5:22\endash 33)</li>\par
            <li>Procreation (Raising godly offspring)</li>\par
            <li>Mutual Sanctification (Building holiness over mere happiness)</li>\par
          </ul>\par
        </p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. Biblical Roles and Health</p>\par
        <p>Biblical order implies responsibility, not inequality. <strong>Husbands</strong> are called to sacrificial leadership and love; <strong>Wives</strong> are called to respect and support. Mutual submission (Ephesians 5:21) is the foundation.</p>\par
        <p><strong>Keys to Success:</strong> Honest communication, resolving conflict before sleep, spiritual/physical intimacy, and financial transparency.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: PREPARING FOR MARRIAGE</p>\par
        <p>Marriage doesn't fix immaturity; it exposes it. <strong>Preparation</strong> involves spiritual readiness, sexual discipline, and choosing a spouse wisely (avoiding being unequally yoked, 2 Corinthians 6:14).</p>\par
        <p><strong>Discussion Topics:</strong> Before marriage, discuss children, finances, career goals, and extended family boundaries to avoid surprises.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: PARENTING PRINCIPLES</p>\par
        <p>Children are a heritage and a stewardship (Psalm 127), not possessions. Parents must model faith, as children imitate more than they listen. \par
          <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
            <li><strong>Discipline:</strong> Correction rooted in love, not anger or abuse (Ephesians 6:4).</li>\par
            <li><strong>Training:</strong> Establishing family devotions and prayer habits.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Care:</strong> Validating feelings and providing emotional security.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
        </p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART IV: SINGLENESS WITH PURPOSE</p>\par
        <p>Singleness is a calling of undivided devotion (1 Corinthians 7), not a deficiency or a "waiting room." It is a season to build character, develop skills, and heal past wounds. Healthy singles become healthy spouses.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL IMPLEMENTATION PLAN</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Singles:</strong> Establish sexual boundaries and seek mentorship.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Engaged:</strong> Attend premarital counseling and create a financial plan.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Married:</strong> Weekly check-ins and joint prayer time.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Parents:</strong> Weekly family devotion and intentional one-on-one time with children.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Is my understanding of marriage shaped by Scripture or culture?</li>\par
          <li>How does my home currently reflect the character of Christ?</li>\par
          <li>If single, am I living purposefully or passively?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Married/Engaged:</strong> Have a 30-minute honest conversation about one "unspoken expectation."</li>\par
          <li><strong>Singles:</strong> Identify one area of personal growth to focus on this month.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Parents:</strong> Lead a 10-minute family prayer or devotion session.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>God builds disciples, disciples build families, and families shape nations. When our homes are aligned with His design, generations are strengthened.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 14, \par
    title: "Lesson 14: Christ-Centered Relationships", \par
    description: "Building connections rooted in biblical love, covenant commitment, and purposeful boundaries.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Relationships shape destiny. Your spiritual growth, emotional health, and calling are all influenced by those you walk with. While sin complicated human connection in Genesis 3, Christ restores it\emdash first with God, then with people. A Christ-centered relationship is built on truth, not just emotion or convenience.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: UNDERSTANDING BIBLICAL RELATIONSHIPS</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. Biblical Love vs. Emotional Impulse</p>\par
        <p>Scripture defines love as <strong>character in action</strong> (1 Corinthians 13), not just a feeling. It is a decision to be patient, kind, and selfless. Biblical love is a commitment before it is chemistry; it includes the strength to offer correction and hold to the truth.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. Covenant vs. Contract</p>\par
        <p>Most modern relationships are <strong>contracts</strong> (conditional and self-protective). Biblical relationships are <strong>covenants</strong>\emdash promise-based, loyalty-driven, and sacrificial. Healthy Christian friendships should reflect this faithfulness rather than mere convenience.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. The Power of Boundaries</p>\par
        <p>Love without boundaries becomes dysfunction. Boundaries are not rejection; they are wisdom. \par
          <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
            <li><strong>Emotional:</strong> Not sharing intimate spaces reserved for a spouse.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Physical:</strong> Maintaining purity in dating.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Time:</strong> Ensuring relationships don't displace your priority with God.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Spiritual:</strong> Guarding against ungodly influence (1 Corinthians 15:33).</li>\par
          </ul>\par
        </p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: SINGLENESS WITH PURPOSE</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. The Gift of Singleness</p>\par
        <p>Singleness is a calling of undivided devotion (1 Corinthians 7), not a "second-class" status. It is a season for focused character development and ministry flexibility. Your value is found in Christ, not your marital status.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. Emotional Purity & Guarding the Heart</p>\par
        <p>Physical purity is only half the battle. Emotional purity means avoiding "almost relationships," flirtation without intention, and awakening love prematurely. <strong>Proverbs 4:23</strong> commands us to guard our hearts as the wellspring of life.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. Preparation Over Pursuit</p>\par
        <p>Marriage does not create maturity; it reveals it. While single, focus on:\par
          <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
            <li><strong>Spiritual Maturity:</strong> Consistent prayer and accountability.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Emotional Health:</strong> Healing past traumas and learning communication.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Financial Responsibility:</strong> Budgeting and generosity.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
        </p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WARNING SIGNS (RED FLAGS)</p>\par
        <p>Love does not ignore manipulation, isolation from community, disrespect, or spiritual compromise. Christ-centered love is wise and discerning.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL APPLICATION</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>For Singles:</strong> Strengthen same-gender friendships and establish clear dating standards.</li>\par
          <li><strong>For Dating:</strong> Involve spiritual mentors and avoid prolonged undefined relationships.</li>\par
          <li><strong>For All:</strong> Practice biblical love daily and forgive quickly.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Are my current relationships drawing me closer to Christ or toward compromise?</li>\par
          <li>How can I set healthier boundaries in my life right now?</li>\par
          <li>What is the difference between emotional purity and physical purity?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Identify one relationship where a boundary needs to be established or strengthened.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize 1 Corinthians 15:33.</li>\par
          <li>Spend 15 minutes in prayer asking God to reveal any "red flags" you've been ignoring in your associations.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>Christ-centered relationships are cultivated through submission to Him. When Christ is central, singleness becomes purposeful, dating becomes intentional, and friendship becomes a tool for sharpening one another.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 15, \par
    title: "Lesson 15: Courtship & Pre-Marital Wisdom", \par
    description: "Navigating the transition from singleness to covenant through intentional evaluation and biblical discernment.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Choosing a spouse is one of life's most significant decisions. It is not merely about romance; it is choosing a lifelong covenant partner, a spiritual companion, and a ministry co-laborer. Courtship is the intentional evaluation of that partnership guided by wisdom (Proverbs 4:7).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: HOW TO CHOOSE A SPOUSE</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">1. Character Over Chemistry</p>\par
        <p>Chemistry attracts, but <strong>character sustains</strong>. While attraction starts the journey, traits like integrity, humility, and teachability determine if the relationship survives trials. Look for the "virtuous spouse" patterns found in Proverbs 31.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">2. Spiritual Alignment</p>\par
        <p>The command is clear: <em>\ldblquote Do not be unequally yoked\rdblquote </em> (2 Corinthians 6:14). This goes beyond cultural Christianity. True compatibility means a shared faith in Christ, shared submission to Scripture, and shared moral convictions. You are joining spiritual directions, not just personalities.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">3. Observation and Counsel</p>\par
        <p>Time reveals truth. Observe how they handle stress, treat service workers, and manage money. Involve spiritual leaders and mentors; isolation clouds judgment, but wisdom grows through counsel (Proverbs 11:14).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: RED FLAGS & GREEN LIGHTS</p>\par
        <p className="italic text-sm text-red-600">Red Flags (Warning Indicators):</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Spiritual Indifference:</strong> No personal prayer life or hunger for the Word.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Lack of Accountability:</strong> Defensive when confronted; rejects correction.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Emotional Instability:</strong> Extreme jealousy, control, or explosive anger.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Unrepentant Patterns:</strong> Sexual compromise or financial irresponsibility without a plan for change.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="italic text-sm text-green-600 pt-2">Green Lights (Maturity Indicators):</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Consistency:</strong> Faithful to God and the church community.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Emotional Maturity:</strong> Takes responsibility for mistakes and handles conflict calmly.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Financial Responsibility:</strong> Practicing budgeting, saving, and giving.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Honor:</strong> Respects boundaries and speaks kindly of authority.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: FINANCIAL & VISION ALIGNMENT</p>\par
        <p>Money issues are a leading cause of marital conflict. Unity requires transparency and an agreed giving philosophy. Furthermore, <strong>Vision Alignment</strong> is essential. Love cannot compensate for conflicting life directions regarding children, career goals, or ministry involvement. <em>\ldblquote Can two walk together unless they agree?\rdblquote </em> (Amos 3:3).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRE-MARITAL WISDOM PRACTICES</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Complete pre-marital counseling to prevent predictable conflicts.</li>\par
          <li>Establish and protect physical and sexual boundaries.</li>\par
          <li>Discuss expectations regarding extended family and roles early.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">QUESTIONS TO ASK BEFORE ENGAGEMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 italic">\par
          <li>"How do you handle anger and stress?"</li>\par
          <li>"What role will our extended families play in our lives?"</li>\par
          <li>"What are your specific spiritual disciplines and life goals?"</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL 30-DAY PLAN</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> List your non-negotiables based on Scripture, not preference.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> Evaluate your current relationship (or readiness) against the Green Light list.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> Have a transparent financial discussion with your partner or mentor.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Seek a mature married couple for a mentorship session.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>Courtship is about discerning covenant partnership. Choose character over charm and alignment over attraction. A wise choice builds a generational blessing, while a careless one creates preventable hardship. Choose prayerfully and patiently.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
