\{ \par
    id: 16, \par
    title: "Lesson 16: Marriage as a Covenant", \par
    description: "Shifting from a contract mindset to a divine covenant based on the three pillars of Genesis 2:24.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Marriage is not a social experiment; it is a divine covenant established in Genesis 2 and reaffirmed by Jesus (Matthew 19:4\endash 6). While a contract protects personal rights, a covenant binds hearts in a promise before God. This understanding transforms how we handle conflict, intimacy, and roles.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: GOD\rquote S DESIGN (GENESIS 2:24)</p>\par
        <p>Genesis 2:24 outlines the three essential pillars of the marital structure:</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Leaving:</strong> Establishing emotional and practical independence from parents.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Cleaving:</strong> A permanent covenant attachment and loyalty to one's spouse.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Becoming One Flesh:</strong> Spiritual, emotional, and physical unity in a shared destiny.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p>Marriage is theological before it is emotional\emdash it reflects Christ\rquote s love for His Church (Ephesians 5:22\endash 33).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: ROLES & RESPONSIBILITIES</p>\par
        <p className="font-semibold">1. The Husband\rquote s Role</p>\par
        <p>Called to love sacrificially as Christ loved the Church (Ephesians 5:25). This includes spiritual leadership: initiating prayer, guarding doctrine, and modeling repentance. Leadership is accountability, not control.</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-2">2. The Wife\rquote s Role</p>\par
        <p>Called to respect and support, acting as a "helper suitable" (Genesis 2:18). This "help" reflects God\rquote s own nature of strengthening His people. A wise woman builds her house through responsive partnership (Proverbs 14:1).</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-2">3. Mutual Submission</p>\par
        <p>Submission begins with mutual humility (Ephesians 5:21). Both must forgive quickly, communicate honestly, and protect their shared unity.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: COMMUNICATION & CONFLICT RESOLUTION</p>\par
        <p>Conflict is inevitable, but destruction is optional. Speak truth in love (Ephesians 4:15) and be quick to listen (James 1:19). Avoid contempt\emdash never use mockery or character attacks.</p>\par
        <p><strong>Covenant Resolution:</strong> Address issues early to prevent bitterness. Focus on the issue ("I feel hurt when..."), not the identity ("You always..."). Seek restoration, not victory; marriage is not a courtroom.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART IV: INTIMACY & SEXUAL PURITY</p>\par
        <p>The marriage bed is honorable (Hebrews 13:4). Sex is designed for bonding, pleasure, and covenant renewal. 1 Corinthians 7 teaches mutual responsibility in intimacy\emdash it should never be weaponized for control or punishment. Purity involves guarding against pornography and maintaining emotional exclusivity.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART V: MANAGING EXTERNAL INTERFERENCE</p>\par
        <p>Marriage creates a new primary loyalty. While we must honor parents (Exodus 20:12), it does not mean allowing interference or manipulation. Establish healthy boundaries regarding finances and private decision-making. <strong>Unity is protection:</strong> never criticize your spouse to outsiders or parents.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL COVENANT HABITS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Weekly:</strong> Intentional conversation and shared prayer time.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> Financial review and vision alignment check-in.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Annually:</strong> A marriage retreat or renewal time to revisit shared goals.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Do I currently treat my marriage (or future marriage) as a covenant or a convenience?</li>\par
          <li>How can we better "leave and cleave" regarding our extended families?</li>\par
          <li>Is our communication marked by grace or by a desire to "win" arguments?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Practice "Quick Listening"\emdash let your spouse speak fully before you respond.</li>\par
          <li>Identify one external influence that may be interfering with your marital unity and set a boundary.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Genesis 2:24.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>A strong marriage is built on daily covenant faithfulness, not emotion alone. When both spouses submit to Christ and honor biblical order, their home becomes a testimony to the world of Christ\rquote s covenant love.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 17, \par
    title: "Lesson 17: Parenting & Family Discipleship", \par
    description: "Shifting from behavior modification to spiritual formation, modeling faith, and establishing a godly home culture.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Parenting is more than raising well-behaved children; it is forming disciples. Parents are the primary spiritual influence (Deuteronomy 6:6\endash 7). We are stewards of children who ultimately belong to God, entrusted with their spiritual and moral formation.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: RAISING GODLY CHILDREN</p>\par
        <p>The ultimate goal of biblical parenting is spiritual maturity and a personal relationship with Christ (Proverbs 22:6). <strong>Training</strong> implies intentional guidance, not passive hope.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Model Before You Instruct:</strong> Children imitate patterns of prayer, repentance, and respect more than they obey verbal commands.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Balance Truth and Grace:</strong> Correction without warmth produces rebellion; warmth without correction produces disorder (Ephesians 6:4).</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: DISCIPLINE WITH LOVE</p>\par
        <p>Discipline is loving correction, not anger-driven punishment. It aims to shape character and teach responsibility (Proverbs 13:24).</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Be Consistent:</strong> Inconsistency creates confusion.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Be Calm:</strong> Never discipline in uncontrolled anger.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Be Clear:</strong> Ensure the child knows what rule was broken and why the consequence follows.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Restore Relationship:</strong> Reaffirm love and pray together after correction to maintain connection.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: CREATING A GODLY HOME CULTURE</p>\par
        <p>Your home has an atmosphere\emdash either intentional or accidental. To serve the Lord as a household (Joshua 24:15):</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Build a Culture of Honor:</strong> Parents must model respect toward each other to create security for children.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Open Communication:</strong> Create a safe space for hard questions and admitting mistakes. Grace-filled homes heal sin rather than hiding it.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Model Repentance:</strong> When parents apologize for being wrong, it teaches children that humility is a strength.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART IV: THE FAMILY ALTAR</p>\par
        <p>The "family altar" is intentional time for worship and Scripture (Deuteronomy 6). <strong>Consistency matters more than length.</strong></p>\par
        <ul className="list-decimal ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>Worship:</strong> 5\endash 10 minutes of song.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Scripture:</strong> Short, age-appropriate reading.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Discussion:</strong> Ask how the passage applies to daily life.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Prayer:</strong> Let children participate and lead.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART V: HANDLING REBELLION</p>\par
        <p>Distinguish between <strong>immaturity</strong> (developmental mistakes) and <strong>rebellion</strong> (persistent defiance). When rebellion occurs: address it early with firmness and love, increase spiritual engagement through prayer, and maintain the relationship by separating the child's identity from their behavior.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL FAMILY PLAN</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Daily:</strong> Short family prayer or Scripture reference.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Weekly:</strong> A formal "family altar" gathering and one-on-one time with each child.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> A family vision conversation to evaluate growth.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Am I modeling the same faith and character I expect my children to have?</li>\par
          <li>Does my discipline style currently lead toward restoration or distance?</li>\par
          <li>What is one way we can make "Family Altar" time more engaging this week?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Hold at least one family devotion session this week.</li>\par
          <li>If you\rquote ve been harsh or wrong lately, practice modeling repentance by apologizing to your child.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Deuteronomy 6:6\endash 7.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>A godly home is built intentionally, one day at a time. Children may not remember every rule, but they will remember the atmosphere of love and the consistency of your example.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
