\{ \par
    id: 20, \par
    title: "Lesson 20: Business & Marketplace Integrity", \par
    description: "Transforming the workplace into a mission field through ethical excellence and breaking financial strongholds.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>The marketplace is more than a place to earn a living\emdash it is a mission field. God calls His disciples to live out their faith in the arena of commerce (Colossians 3:23\endash 24). This final lesson focuses on operating with unshakeable integrity and overcoming the mental and practical strongholds that hinder financial freedom.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: ETHICS & INTEGRITY IN BUSINESS</p>\par
        <p>Business is not a "neutral" zone; it is a reflection of your heart. Integrity is non-negotiable: <em>\ldblquote Better is a poor person who walks in integrity than a rich person who is crooked\rdblquote </em> (Proverbs 28:6).</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Common Challenges:</strong> Cutting corners for profit, falsifying records, or misrepresenting services.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Kingdom Standards:</strong> Develop transparent accounting, honor contracts fully, and refuse all forms of bribery or corruption.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: FAITH AT WORK</p>\par
        <p>Your work, whether digital development or property management, is an act of worship. <strong>Excellence</strong> honors God and opens doors for the Gospel that words alone cannot. Use your professional platform to:\par
          <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
            <li>Deliver quality consistently and meet every deadline.</li>\par
            <li>Speak truth with grace to clients and colleagues.</li>\par
            <li>Solve problems with a spirit of service, not just for a paycheck.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
        </p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: BREAKING FINANCIAL STRONGHOLDS</p>\par
        <p>Many believers are held back by internal strongholds that prevent them from stewarding wealth effectively.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li><strong>The Poverty Mentality:</strong> A mindset of scarcity (Proverbs 23:7). Transformation begins by realizing God is your unlimited source.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Financial Fear:</strong> Leads to hoarding or impulsive decisions. Combat this with the promise of Philippians 4:19.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Sloth & Mismanagement:</strong> Proverbs 6:6\endash 11 warns against laziness. Discipline, planning, and continuous skill acquisition are the keys to breaking these chains.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        \par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PRACTICAL STEPS TO STABILITY</p>\par
        <ul className="list-decimal ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Diversify Income:</strong> Develop multiple streams by leveraging your unique skills.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Eliminate Debt:</strong> Prioritize the repayment of high-interest loans to regain your freedom.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Invest Wisely:</strong> Seek counsel and practice patience; sustainable wealth is built slowly.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Generosity:</strong> Tithing breaks the spiritual bondage of greed.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Am I currently compromising any ethical principles for short-term financial gain?</li>\par
          <li>How can I improve the "Excellence" of my current business or job to better reflect Christ?</li>\par
          <li>Which financial stronghold\emdash fear, scarcity, or mismanagement\emdash do I need to confront today?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Identify one "shortcut" you've been tempted to take in business and commit to the honest path.</li>\par
          <li>Review your business or professional goals: do they include impact or just income?</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Colossians 3:23.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>Marketplace integrity is a disciple's altar. Your business is not just an income source\emdash it is a platform for Kingdom impact. Live faithfully, pursue excellence, and break every stronghold in Christ\rquote s name.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 21, \par
    title: "Lesson 21: Emotional & Mental Wholeness", \par
    description: "Integrating spiritual maturity with emotional health, healing from past trauma, and understanding mental health through a biblical lens.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Spiritual maturity and emotional health are partners, not enemies. One can be biblically literate yet emotionally reactive or wounded. God cares for the whole person\emdash spirit, soul, and body (1 Thessalonians 5:23). Wholeness is not a sign of weakness; it is a matter of stewardship.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: EMOTIONAL INTELLIGENCE FOR BELIEVERS</p>\par
        <p>Emotions are signals, not sins. Jesus expressed sorrow, righteous anger, and wept (John 11:35). Emotions become destructive only when they are unprocessed or allowed to override truth.</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-2">1. Managing Anger & Jealousy</p>\par
        <p>Anger itself is not sin, but unmanaged anger leads to bitterness (Ephesians 4:26). Identifying triggers and slowing down (James 1:19) is key. Similarly, jealousy often stems from a performance-based identity; healing comes from anchoring your security in Christ rather than comparison.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: HEALING FROM TRAUMA & PAST PAIN</p>\par
        <p>Trauma is an unprocessed, overwhelming experience. God heals the brokenhearted (Psalm 147:3), but healing requires an intentional path:</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Rejection:</strong> Replace the lies of being "unwanted" with the truth of your sonship in God.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Betrayal:</strong> Forgive intentionally, while understanding that forgiveness does not always mean the immediate removal of boundaries.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Abuse:</strong> Acknowledge the harm, establish safety, and seek professional help. Spiritualizing abuse without ensuring safety is dangerous.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Grief:</strong> Allow yourself to feel; suppressed grief often leads to depression.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        \par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: MENTAL HEALTH & FAITH</p>\par
        <p>Depression and anxiety are not always signs of weak faith or demonic oppression; they can involve biological factors, chronic stress, or trauma. Figures like Elijah and David experienced deep despair (1 Kings 19, Psalms).</p>\par
        \par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-2">When to Seek Help</p>\par
        <p>Therapy is not unbelief\emdash it is wisdom. Seek professional help if symptoms interfere with daily functioning, appetite, or sleep for extended periods. God heals through prayer, the renewal of the mind (Romans 12:2), and the skills of professionals like physicians (Luke was a physician!).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL FRAMEWORK</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Daily:</strong> Practice emotional "check-ins" and honest journaling before God.</li>\par
          <li><strong>When Triggered:</strong> Pause before reacting, identify the core wound, and respond according to truth rather than impulse.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Do I tend to suppress my emotions or explode when under pressure?</li>\par
          <li>In what areas have I confused emotional struggle with spiritual failure?</li>\par
          <li>Is there a "past pain" that still dictates how I react to people today?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Start a daily "Honesty Journal"\emdash write down three emotions you felt today and why.</li>\par
          <li>Identify one person you need to forgive, not because they deserve it, but for your own wholeness.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Proverbs 4:23.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>Spiritual maturity is not emotional numbness; it is emotional submission to truth. You can love God deeply and seek therapy wisely. Healing is a process, and God is not ashamed of your wounds\emdash He enters them.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 22, \par
    title: "Lesson 22: Social Responsibility & Cultural Engagement", \par
    description: "Navigating dual citizenship, biblical justice, and the believer's role as salt and light in modern society.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Discipleship isn't confined to the four walls of a church; it extends into the heart of society. As "salt" and "light" (Matthew 5:13\endash 16), believers are called to preserve moral order and expose darkness. We are citizens of Heaven first, but we are also stewards of our earthly nations.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: CHRISTIAN CITIZENSHIP</p>\par
        <p>Scripture shows heroes like Joseph, Daniel, and Paul engaging deeply with secular systems without losing their identity. <strong>Kingdom First:</strong> While we respect our earthly nations, political loyalty must never replace our loyalty to Christ. We engage culture through discernment, not blind nationalism.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: OBEYING AUTHORITIES</p>\par
        <p>Romans 13:1\endash 7 mandates submission to governing authorities as an act of honoring God\rquote s established order. This includes paying taxes and respecting law enforcement. However, this obedience is not absolute. If a law directly contradicts God's commands, we must follow the example of the Apostles: <em>\ldblquote We must obey God rather than men\rdblquote </em> (Acts 5:29).</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: VOTING & CIVIC RESPONSIBILITY</p>\par
        <p>While modern democracy isn't in the Bible, the principle of <strong>stewardship of influence</strong> is. Voting is a way to "seek the welfare of the city" (Jeremiah 29:7).</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>How to Vote:</strong> Evaluate moral alignment with biblical principles, integrity of character, and policies affecting life and freedom\emdash rather than just party or ethnic loyalty.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Beyond the Ballot:</strong> True engagement includes community service, advocacy for the vulnerable, and running for office if called.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART IV: SOCIAL JUSTICE FROM A BIBLICAL LENS</p>\par
        <p>Biblical justice is rooted in God's character, not political ideology. Micah 6:8 calls us to <em>\ldblquote act justly, love mercy, and walk humbly.\rdblquote </em></p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Biblical Priorities:</strong> Protecting the poor, the widow, the orphan, and ensuring fair judgment for all (James 1:27).</li>\par
          <li><strong>The Balance:</strong> Biblical justice balances personal responsibility with structural fairness. It seeks mercy without compromising truth.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        \par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL SOCIAL ENGAGEMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li>Support local education or poverty alleviation initiatives.</li>\par
          <li>Mentor youth and advocate against workplace or community corruption.</li>\par
          <li>Engage in respectful dialogue with those who hold different political views.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Is my political identity currently stronger or more vocal than my Christian identity?</li>\par
          <li>How can I be "salt" in my specific industry (e.g., tech or property management) this week?</li>\par
          <li>In what ways can I seek the welfare of my local community beyond just paying taxes?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Identify one local social need (e.g., a struggling school or orphanage) and find a way to serve or donate.</li>\par
          <li>Pray specifically for your national and local leaders by name (1 Timothy 2:1\endash 2).</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Micah 6:8.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>The world does not need louder Christians; it needs wiser, holier, and more compassionate ones. Stand for truth courageously, but pursue justice humbly. Your earthly citizenship is a platform for your heavenly witness.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 23, \par
    title: "Lesson 23: Media & Digital Discipline", \par
    description: "Navigating the digital age with a guarded heart, breaking digital addiction, and mastering social media conduct.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>We are the first generation discipled by screens as much as by sermons. Media is not neutral; it shapes our desires, attention spans, and identity. The urgent question for every disciple is: <strong>"Is media using you, or are you using it?"</strong> Digital discipline is a vital form of spiritual warfare.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: SOCIAL MEDIA CONDUCT</p>\par
        <p>Out of the abundance of the heart, the fingers type (Matthew 12:34). Every post is a reflection of your spiritual formation. Before you post, use the <strong>Edification Filter</strong> (Ephesians 4:29):</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li>Does this glorify God or just my ego?</li>\par
          <li>Does this build others up or fuel "outrage culture"?</li>\par
          <li>Would I say this face-to-face?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p>Avoid <em>Performative Christianity</em>\emdash where the platform replaces the prayer closet. Influence must always be governed by character.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: BREAKING DIGITAL ADDICTION</p>\par
        <p>Addiction is compulsive engagement despite harm. If your device masters you, your discipleship is compromised (1 Corinthians 6:12). Platforms are engineered for dopamine spikes that reward distraction and fragment the mind.</p>\par
        \par
        \par
\par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-2">Practical Discipline Plan:</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Phone-Free Zones:</strong> No screens for the first 30 minutes of the day or 60 minutes before bed.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Digital Sabbath:</strong> One day a week with limited or zero social media.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Notifications Audit:</strong> Turn off all non-essential pings to regain your attention.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Replace, Don't Just Remove:</strong> Replace scrolling with Scripture; replace browsing with prayer.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: GUARDING YOUR EYES & MIND</p>\par
        <p>The eye is the lamp of the body (Matthew 6:22\endash 23). What you look at shapes what you desire. This requires a <strong>Covenant with the Eyes</strong> (Job 31:1).</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Pornography:</strong> Rewires the brain and destroys spiritual sensitivity. Break the cycle with radical honesty, accountability partners, and filtering software.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Content Curation:</strong> Avoid "worthless things" (Psalm 101:3) like graphic violence or mockery of holiness that desensitize the soul.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Renewing the Mind:</strong> Transformation requires a steady diet of Scripture, worship, and intentional silence (Romans 12:2).</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WARNING SIGNS OF COMPROMISE</p>\par
        <p className="italic text-sm text-red-600">Irritability when interrupted, secret online habits, declining prayer life, and an increased fantasy life.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>If my browsing history were made public today, would I be comfortable or ashamed?</li>\par
          <li>Do I consume more digital content in a day than I do the Word of God?</li>\par
          <li>How has my attention span for deep prayer been affected by my phone usage?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Check your "Screen Time" settings and identify your top 3 time-wasting apps.</li>\par
          <li>Commit to a 24-hour "Digital Fast" this weekend.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Matthew 6:22\endash 23.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>Your attention is currency and your soul is sacred. Do not let an algorithm disciple you. Guard your gates fiercely and let Christ shape your imagination. Digital discipline is not legalism; it is <strong>liberation</strong>.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
