\{ \par
    id: 25, \par
    title: "Lesson 25: Entrepreneurship, Innovation & Cultural Intelligence", \par
    description: "Multiplying impact through ethical wealth creation, calculated risk, and a global kingdom mindset.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>If disciples are to shape society, they cannot only occupy jobs\emdash they must create systems. Entrepreneurship is stewardship multiplied, innovation is faithful adaptation, and cultural intelligence ensures that our impact can cross borders. This is a strategic necessity for the modern disciple.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: KINGDOM ENTREPRENEURSHIP & INNOVATION</p>\par
        <p>Entrepreneurship is the ability to identify problems and organize resources to solve them. God, the ultimate Creator, gives us the "power to get wealth" (Deuteronomy 8:18) not for pride, but for purpose. Kingdom entrepreneurs create employment, fund missions, and model ethical prosperity.</p>\par
        <p className="italic text-sm">\ldblquote Innovation is not rebellion against tradition; it is faithful multiplication.\rdblquote </p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Multiplication over Preservation:</strong> The parable of the talents (Matthew 25) shows that God rewards those who take what they have and grow it.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Architecting Culture:</strong> Disciples should innovate in tech, healthcare, media, and finance rather than just consuming what the world builds.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: RISK & ETHICAL PROFIT</p>\par
        <p>Faith is not recklessness, and caution is not cowardice. Biblical wisdom encourages diversification: <em>\ldblquote Invest in seven ventures, yes, in eight\rdblquote </em> (Ecclesiastes 11:2). Managing risk involves research, counsel (Proverbs 11:14), and starting lean.</p>\par
        <p className="font-semibold pt-2">What is Ethical Profit?</p>\par
        <p>Profit is a tool, not a sin. Ethical profit includes fair wages, honest pricing, and transparent contracts. Kingdom economics ensures that wealth remains a servant, never a master.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: CULTURAL INTELLIGENCE (CQ)</p>\par
        <p>The Kingdom of God is transnational, including "every tribe, tongue, and nation" (Revelation 7:9). Cultural Intelligence is the ability to engage across these boundaries without ethnocentrism\emdash the false belief that one's own culture is superior.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Adaptability:</strong> Following Paul\rquote s example of becoming "all things to all people" (1 Corinthians 9:22) to win some.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Global Partnership:</strong> Recognizing that the center of Christianity has shifted globally (Africa, Asia, Latin America) and learning from diverse expressions of faith.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">PRACTICAL STEPS FOR GROWTH</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Entrepreneurship:</strong> Solve a problem in your community or industry rather than just seeking a paycheck.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Risk:</strong> Identify one "calculated risk" you\rquote ve avoided out of fear and evaluate it prayerfully.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Culture:</strong> Build a friendship or professional partnership with someone from a different ethnic or national background.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Am I currently creating solutions or just consuming resources?</li>\par
          <li>In my business or career, am I operating with "Cultural Intelligence" or assuming my way is the only way?</li>\par
          <li>Is my current financial model sustainable and ethical?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Research one global market or culture different from your own to understand their values.</li>\par
          <li>Draft a "Kingdom Innovation" idea\emdash a solution to a problem you see in your current field.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Deuteronomy 8:18.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>The church gathers on Sunday, but entrepreneurs shape Monday. When disciples build creatively, profit ethically, and engage globally, they unite nations under Christ. Your business is not just an income\emdash it is a global kingdom asset.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\{ \par
    id: 26, \par
    title: "Lesson 26: Personal Leadership & Self-Mastery", \par
    description: "Governing your soul, time, and habits to build a foundation for public influence and private peace.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-2">\par
        <p className="font-bold">INTRODUCTION</p>\par
        <p>Before you lead others, you must lead yourself. The greatest leadership failure is not public collapse, but private disorder. Personal leadership is the ability to govern your time, discipline your desires, and execute your vision consistently. As Proverbs 25:28 warns, a person without self-control is like a city with broken walls\emdash defenseless and disordered.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART I: TIME & ENERGY STEWARDSHIP</p>\par
        <p>Time is a non-renewable resource; once spent, it cannot be reclaimed (Ephesians 5:15\endash 16). Effective self-leadership isn't about managing time, but managing <em>yourself</em> within time.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Weekly Planning:</strong> Define your top 3 priorities. If it isn't scheduled, it's accidental.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Energy Management:</strong> Identify your peak mental hours (deep work) and protect them from shallow distractions.</li>\par
          <li><strong>The Theology of "Numbering":</strong> Psalm 90:12 teaches that numbering our days produces a heart of wisdom and urgency.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART II: PRIORITIES & THE POWER OF FOCUS</p>\par
        <p>Activity is not accomplishment. Distraction destroys depth, while focus is sustained attention toward a defined objective. Follow the Nehemiah Principle: <em>\ldblquote I am doing a great work and cannot come down\rdblquote </em> (Nehemiah 6:3). Learning to say "No" to the good allows you to say "Yes" to the great.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART III: HABIT FORMATION & DISCIPLINE</p>\par
        <p>Discipline does not imprison; it liberates. You do not rise to the level of your intentions; you fall to the level of your systems. Habits are automated behaviors that compound over time into spiritual maturity or financial stability.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Spiritual Habits:</strong> Scripture, prayer, and fasting\emdash consistency over intensity.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Physical Habits:</strong> Sleep, diet, and exercise. Your body supports your calling.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Habit Formula:</strong> Start small, attach to existing routines, and track consistency.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART IV: BREAKING DESTRUCTIVE CYCLES</p>\par
        <p>Cycles like procrastination, anger, or negative thinking are reinforced by belief. Transformation requires the <strong>Renewal of the Mind</strong> (Romans 12:2). Admitting the pattern, identifying triggers, and building external accountability are the practical steps to breaking these chains.</p>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-4 text-lg">PART V: VISION & STRATEGIC PLANNING</p>\par
        <p>Where there is no vision, people perish (Proverbs 29:18). Vision provides the direction; goals provide the execution.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-decimal ml-5 text-sm">\par
          <li><strong>Personal Vision:</strong> Write a concise statement answering: <em>Who am I becoming?</em></li>\par
          <li><strong>SMART Goals:</strong> Ensure goals are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Annual Audit:</strong> Once a year, reflect deeply, eliminate "dead weight," and adjust your 3\endash 5 year objectives.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Do I currently control my schedule, or does my schedule (and notifications) control me?</li>\par
          <li>What is one recurring destructive cycle in my life that I need to confront with a new "system"?</li>\par
          <li>Is my daily "To-Do" list actually aligned with my 5-year vision?</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">WEEKLY ASSIGNMENT</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc ml-5">\par
          <li>Write down your "Personal Vision Statement" and place it where you see it daily.</li>\par
          <li>Identify your "Peak Energy Window" and schedule 90 minutes of uninterrupted deep work during that time for 5 days.</li>\par
          <li>Memorize Proverbs 25:28.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p className="font-bold pt-2">CONCLUSION</p>\par
        <p>No one accidentally fulfills their destiny. Master yourself, order your days, and guard your focus. When you lead yourself well, your leadership in the marketplace and society becomes a natural, powerful overflow.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
