\f0\lang1033 Baptismal Modules:\par
\{ \par
    id: 1, \par
    title: "Lesson 1: THE MEANING OF SALVATION", \par
    description: "Understanding sin, God's redemptive plan, and salvation by grace.",\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">1. WHAT IS SALVATION?</h4>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">A. Definition of Salvation</h5>\par
          <p>Salvation means deliverance, rescue, preservation, and restoration from sin and its consequences.</p>\par
          <p>In the New Testament, the Greek word soteria means:</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Rescue from danger</li>\par
            <li>Deliverance from destruction</li>\par
            <li>Restoration to wholeness</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <p>Salvation is not merely joining a church. It is not moral reform. It is not religion.</p>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="Matthew 1:21, Luke 19:10, Hebrews 7:25, Ephesians 2:8\f1\endash 9, Titus 3:5" /></p>\par
          <p>Salvation is God rescuing man from sin and restoring him into relationship with Himself through Jesus Christ.</p>\par
        </div>\par
\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">2. WHAT IS SIN?</h4>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">A. Definition of Sin</h5>\par
          <p>Sin is:</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Disobedience to God</li>\par
            <li>Rebellion against God\rquote s authority</li>\par
            <li>Missing the mark of God\rquote s standard</li>\par
            <li>Living independently of God</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <p>The Greek word hamartia means "to miss the mark."</p>\par
          <p>Sin is not just bad behavior \emdash  It is a condition of the heart.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">B. Three Dimensions of Sin</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Inherited sin (Sin nature) - We were born with a sinful nature.</li>\par
            <li>Imputed sin (Adam\rquote s fall credited to humanity)</li>\par
            <li>Personal sin (Our individual actions)</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">C. Root of Sin</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Pride</li>\par
            <li>Self-will</li>\par
            <li>Unbelief</li>\par
            <li>Desire to be independent of God</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="John 3:4, Romans 3:23, James 4:17, Romans 14:23, Psalms 51:5, Romans 5:12, Ephesians 2:1\endash 3" /></p>\par
        </div>\par
\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">3. THE FALL OF MAN (Genesis 3 Overview)</h4>\par
          <p>\f2\u-10179?\u-9002?\f1  \f0 Found in Book of Genesis chapter 3.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">A. The Setting</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>God created man in His image.</li>\par
            <li>Man had fellowship with God.</li>\par
            <li>Man was given dominion and responsibility.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">B. The Temptation</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>The serpent questioned God\f1\rquote\f0 s word.</li>\par
            <li>Doubt was introduced.</li>\par
            <li>Eve was deceived; Adam willfully disobeyed.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">C. What Actually Happened?</h5>\par
          <p>It was not just eating fruit. It was:</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Distrust in God</li>\par
            <li>Rejection of divine authority</li>\par
            <li>Choosing self over obedience</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">D. The Immediate Effects</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Shame</li>\par
            <li>Fear</li>\par
            <li>Hiding from God</li>\par
            <li>Blame shifting</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">E. The Spiritual Reality</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Spiritual death occurred.</li>\par
            <li>Separation from God.</li>\par
            <li>The sin nature entered humanity.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="Genesis 1:26\f1\endash 27, Genesis 2:15\endash 17, Genesis 3:1\endash 6, 2 Corinthians 11:3, Genesis 2:17, Romans 5:12, Romans 6:23, Genesis 3:7\endash 10, Isaiah 59:2" /></p>\par
        </div>\par
\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">4. CONSEQUENCES OF SIN</h4>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="Isaiah 59:1\endash 2, Colossians 1:21, Ephesians 2:1, 1 Timothy 5:6, Romans 5:12, Hebrews 9:27, John 8:34, Romans 6:16\endash 20, Romans 6:23, Revelation 20:12\endash 15" /></p>\par
          <p>Sin carries consequences both spiritual and practical.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">A. Separation from God</h5>\par
          <p>Sin creates a barrier between man and God.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">B. Spiritual Death</h5>\par
          <p>Not physical death first \emdash  but spiritual disconnection.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">C. Physical Death</h5>\par
          <p>Death entered the human race.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">D. Corruption of Human Nature</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Mind darkened</li>\par
            <li>Will weakened</li>\par
            <li>Emotions distorted</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">E. Bondage</h5>\par
          <p>Sin enslaves.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">F. Judgment</h5>\par
          <p>Without redemption, sin leads to eternal separation from God.</p>\par
        </div>\par
\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">5. GOD\rquote S REDEMPTIVE PLAN</h4>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="Genesis 3:15, Genesis 3:21, Leviticus 17:11, Hebrews 9:22, Isaiah 53:5\endash 6, Micah 5:2, Ephesians 1:4\endash 5, 1 Peter 1:18\endash 20, Revelation 13:8" /></p>\par
          <p>Salvation was not an afterthought. God had a plan even before the fall.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">A. The Promise in Eden</h5>\par
          <p>God promised the seed of the woman would crush the serpent (Genesis 3:15).</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">B. The Pattern of Sacrifice</h5>\par
          <p>Animals were killed to cover Adam and Eve. Blood sacrifice becomes the pattern.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">C. The Covenant System</h5>\par
          <p>Throughout the Old Testament: Abrahamic covenant, Mosaic law, Sacrificial system. All pointed forward to Christ.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">D. Prophetic Announcement</h5>\par
          <p>The prophets foretold a coming Redeemer.</p>\par
        </div>\par
\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">6. THE FINISHED WORK OF CHRIST</h4>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="John 1:1, 14, Colossians 2:9, Hebrews 4:15, Isaiah 53:4\endash 6, 2 Corinthians 5:21, 1 Peter 2:24, Matthew 28:5\endash 6, Romans 4:25, 1 Corinthians 15:3\endash 4, John 19:30, Hebrews 10:10\endash 14" /></p>\par
          <p>Salvation is centered on Jesus Christ.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">A. Who is Jesus?</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Fully God</li>\par
            <li>Fully man</li>\par
            <li>Sinless</li>\par
            <li>The promised Messiah</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">B. His Mission</h5>\par
          <p>To seek and save the lost.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">C. His Death</h5>\par
          <p>On the cross: He bore our sins. He became our substitute. He satisfied divine justice.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">D. His Resurrection</h5>\par
          <p>Victory over death. Proof that the sacrifice was accepted. Declaration of new life.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">E. \ldblquote It Is Finished\rdblquote </h5>\par
          <p>The work of redemption was completed. Nothing can be added to it. Salvation is based on what Christ has done \emdash  not what we do.</p>\par
        </div>\par
\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">7. GRACE VS WORKS</h4>\par
          <p>This is very important for baptismal candidates.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">A. What is Grace?</h5>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:8\endash 9, Romans 3:24, Titus 3:5, Romans 11:6, Galatians 2:16, Ephesians 2:10, James 2:17\endash 18" /></p>\par
          <p>Grace is: God\rquote s unearned favor, Unmerited kindness, Divine empowerment. You cannot earn salvation.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">B. What Are Works?</h5>\par
          <p>Works are: Human effort, Religious activity, Moral behavior.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">C. The Proper Order</h5>\par
          <p>Wrong Order: Works \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Earn salvation</p>\par
          <p>Correct Biblical Order: Grace \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Salvation \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Good works</p>\par
          <p>Good works are the result of salvation \f1\emdash  not the cause of it.</p>\par
          <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">D. Why Works Cannot Save</h5>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Human righteousness is imperfect.</li>\par
            <li>Sin requires divine payment.</li>\par
            <li>Only Christ\rquote s sacrifice satisfies justice.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
        </div>\par
        \par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg mt-4">TEACHING APPLICATION SECTION</h4>\par
          <p>After teaching this module, ask:</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Do you understand what sin is?</li>\par
            <li>Do you believe Jesus died for your sins?</li>\par
            <li>Have you personally accepted Him?</li>\par
            <li>Are you trusting in grace or in your works?</li>\par
          </ul>\par
        </div>\par
\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg mt-4">CLASS DISCUSSION QUESTIONS</h4>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
            <li>Why is salvation more than joining a church?</li>\par
            <li>How does understanding sin deepen gratitude for grace?</li>\par
            <li>Why must salvation be by grace and not works?</li>\par
          </ul>\par
        </div>\par
        \par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg mt-4">SUMMARY STATEMENT FOR STUDENTS</h4>\par
          <p>Salvation is God\rquote s loving rescue plan for humanity, accomplished through the finished work of Jesus Christ, received by grace through faith, and resulting in a transformed life.</p>\par
        </div>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
  \{\par
    id: 2,\par
    title: 'Lesson 2: REPENTANCE',\par
    description: 'A doctrinal and transformational study on repentance.',\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">I. THE PLACE OF REPENTANCE IN THE GOSPEL</h4>\par
          <p>Repentance is not an optional emotional response to preaching. It is an essential element of the gospel call. When Jesus began His ministry, His first recorded message was: <ScriptureText text="\ldblquote Repent and believe in the gospel\rdblquote  (Mark 1:14\endash 15)" />. Repentance was not a side instruction\emdash it was central to the Kingdom announcement.</p>\par
          <p>After His resurrection, Jesus declared that <ScriptureText text="\ldblquote repentance and forgiveness of sins should be preached in His name to all nations\rdblquote  (Luke 24:47)" />. Therefore: Repentance is Christ-commanded. Repentance is apostolic preaching. Repentance is required for forgiveness.</p>\par
          <p>Peter followed this pattern in <ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38: \ldblquote\f0 Repent and be baptized\f1\'85 \f0 for the forgiveness of your sins.\f1\rdblquote " /> Paul affirmed the same universal demand in <ScriptureText text="Acts 17:30" />\emdash God now commands all people everywhere to repent. Repentance is not merely advice. It is a divine command.</p>\par
        </div>\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">II. THE MEANING OF REPENTANCE (METANOIA)</h4>\par
          <p>The Greek word metanoia comes from: meta \endash  after, change; nous \endash  mind. It literally means a change of mind. However, biblically, it is far deeper than intellectual adjustment. In Scripture, the \ldblquote mind\rdblquote  represents one\rquote s worldview, moral judgment, and governing disposition. Therefore, repentance is: A change in how one thinks about sin. A change in how one sees God. A change in one\rquote s moral alignment. A decisive inward reorientation. It is not behavior modification; it is inner transformation.</p>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="Romans 12:2" /> speaks of transformation by the renewing of the mind. While that verse addresses sanctification, it reflects the same principle: true spiritual change begins internally. In the Old Testament, repentance is expressed as \ldblquote returning\rdblquote  to the Lord (<ScriptureText text="Isaiah 55:6\endash 7; Ezekiel 18:30\endash 32" />). It is covenant language. It assumes relationship and speaks of restoration.</p>\par
          <p>Therefore: Repentance is a Spirit-produced inward change of mind and heart that results in a decisive turning from sin and a turning toward God.</p>\par
        </div>\par
        <div>\par
          <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">III. THE NECESSITY OF REPENTANCE</h4>\par
          <p>Repentance is necessary because of three realities:</p>\par
          <ol className="list-decimal pl-5">\par
            <li><strong>The Universality of Sin:</strong> <ScriptureText text="\ldblquote All have sinned and fall short of the glory of God\rdblquote  (Romans 3:23)" />. Sin is not selective; it is universal. Therefore, repentance is not for \ldblquote bad people\rdblquote\emdash it is for all people.</li>\par
            <li><strong>The Seriousness of Sin:</strong> <ScriptureText text="\ldblquote The wages of sin is death\rdblquote  (Romans 6:23)" />. Sin is not merely mistake\emdash it produces death: spiritual separation now and eternal judgment later. Jesus Himself warned, <ScriptureText text="\ldblquote Unless you repent, you will all likewise perish\rdblquote  (Luke 13:3)" />. That is not symbolic language\emdash it is judicial language.</li>\par
            <li><strong>The Holiness of God:</strong> God is holy (<ScriptureText text="Isaiah 6:3" />). <ScriptureText text="Habakkuk 1:13" /> declares that His eyes are too pure to look upon evil. Therefore, repentance is required because God\rquote s holiness demands moral alignment. Repentance is necessary not because God is harsh, but because He is holy.</li>\par
          </ol>\par
        </div>\par
        <div>\par
            <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">IV. GODLY SORROW VS WORLDLY SORROW</h4>\par
            <p>The clearest distinction is given in <ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 7:9\endash 10" />, where Paul says: \ldblquote\f0 Godly sorrow produces repentance leading to salvation\f1\'85 \f0 but worldly sorrow produces death.\f1\rdblquote  This text reveals that sorrow itself is not repentance. It produces repentance.</p>\par
            <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">1. Worldly Sorrow</h5>\par
            <p>Worldly sorrow is grief over consequences, not over sin itself. Judas in <ScriptureText text="Matthew 27:3\endash 5" /> felt remorse. The Greek word used there (metamelomai) means regret, not repentance. He regretted what happened\emdash but he did not turn to Christ. Worldly sorrow: Is self-centered. Focuses on shame or exposure. Fears punishment more than it hates sin. Does not produce transformation. It leads to despair.</p>\par
            <h5 className="font-semibold mt-2">2. Godly Sorrow</h5>\par
            <p>Godly sorrow is grief over having sinned against God. David expressed this in <ScriptureText text="Psalm 51:4: \ldblquote Against You, You only, have I sinned.\rdblquote " /> Though he sinned against Bathsheba and Uriah, he understood the ultimate offense was against God. Godly sorrow: Sees sin as rebellion against a holy God. Produces humility. Leads to confession. Results in transformation. Godly sorrow leads to life.</p>\par
        </div>\par
        <div>\par
            <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">V. THE COMPONENTS OF TRUE REPENTANCE</h4>\par
            <p>Biblical repentance has identifiable elements.</p>\par
            <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">\par
                <li><strong>Conviction:</strong> Jesus said the Holy Spirit would convict the world concerning sin (<ScriptureText text="John 16:8" />). In <ScriptureText text="Acts 2:37" />, after Peter preached, the people were \ldblquote cut to the heart.\rdblquote  Conviction is not emotional hype. It is spiritual illumination. The sinner sees: The seriousness of sin. The holiness of God. The need for mercy. Without conviction, repentance is superficial.</li>\par
                <li><strong>Confession:</strong> <ScriptureText text="\ldblquote If we confess our sins, He is faithful and just to forgive us\rdblquote  (1 John 1:9)" />. Confession means agreeing with God\rquote s verdict. It is not self-justification. <ScriptureText text="Proverbs 28:13" /> says, \ldblquote Whoever conceals his transgressions will not prosper, but he who confesses and forsakes them will obtain mercy.\rdblquote  Notice the order: confess and forsake.</li>\par
                <li><strong>Forsaking Sin (Turning From):</strong> <ScriptureText text="Isaiah 55:7" /> says, \ldblquote\f0 Let the wicked forsake his way\f1\'85 \f0 let him return to the Lord.\f1\rdblquote  Repentance is not merely feeling bad\emdash it involves abandonment of sin. <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:1\endash 2" /> asks, \ldblquote Shall we continue in sin that grace may abound? By no means!\rdblquote  If someone claims repentance but continues deliberately in known sin without struggle or resistance, the repentance is questionable.</li>\par
                <li><strong>Turning to God:</strong> Repentance is not just negative (away from sin), it is positive (toward God). Paul describes conversion in <ScriptureText text="1 Thessalonians 1:9" /> as turning \ldblquote to God from idols to serve the living and true God.\rdblquote  In <ScriptureText text="Acts 26:20" />, Paul preached that people should \ldblquote repent and turn to God, performing deeds in keeping with their repentance.\rdblquote  Repentance changes direction.</li>\par
            </ol>\par
        </div>\par
        <div>\par
            <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">VI. THE FRUITS OF REPENTANCE</h4>\par
            <p>John the Baptist commanded, <ScriptureText text="\ldblquote Bear fruit in keeping with repentance\rdblquote  (Matthew 3:8)" />. Fruit is evidence, not the cause, of repentance. Biblical fruits include: Obedience. Restitution where necessary (<ScriptureText text="Luke 19:8\endash 9" /> \endash  Zacchaeus). New patterns of righteousness. Love for holiness. Hatred for former sin. True repentance alters behavior because it has altered the heart.</p>\par
        </div>\par
        <div>\par
            <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">VII. REPENTANCE AND BAPTISM</h4>\par
            <p>In <ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38" />, repentance precedes baptism. In <ScriptureText text="Acts 8:12\endash 13" />, belief and repentance precede baptism. Baptism symbolizes death to sin (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:3\endash 4" />). Therefore, without repentance, baptism becomes symbolic of something that never occurred. Repentance prepares the heart for baptism.</p>\par
        </div>\par
        <div>\par
            <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">VIII. WHAT REPENTANCE IS NOT</h4>\par
            <p>Repentance is not: Mere emotional crying. Public embarrassment. Temporary reform. Fear-driven confession. Religious performance. Esau sought repentance with tears (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 12:16\endash 17" />), but his heart was not transformed. Repentance is internal surrender, not external drama.</p>\par
        </div>\par
        <div>\par
            <h4 className="font-bold text-lg">CONCLUSION FOR BAPTISMAL CANDIDATES</h4>\par
            <p>Before baptism, a candidate must be able to say: I recognize my sin. I grieve over it because it offends God. I have confessed it. I have turned from it. I am turning toward God in obedience. Repentance is the doorway into the Christian life.</p>\par
        </div>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
  \{\par
    id: 3,\par
    title: 'Lesson 3: FAITH IN JESUS CHRIST',\par
    description: 'Understanding the nature, object, expression, and assurance of saving faith.',\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
        <p><strong>I. THE CENTRALITY OF FAITH IN SALVATION</strong></p>\par
        <p>If repentance is the turning from sin, faith is the turning to Christ. The gospel does not merely call people to stop sinning\emdash it calls them to trust a Savior. Jesus repeatedly declared that eternal life is granted through believing in Him (<ScriptureText text="John 3:16; John 5:24" />). Paul states plainly, <ScriptureText text="\ldblquote By grace you have been saved through faith\rdblquote  (Ephesians 2:8)" />. Therefore, faith is not a religious accessory\emdash it is the instrument through which salvation is received. Grace is the source. Christ is the ground. Faith is the means. Without faith, salvation is not appropriated (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 11:6" />).</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>II. WHAT IS SAVING FAITH?</strong></p>\par
        <p>The New Testament word for faith (pistis) carries the idea of trust, reliance, and confident dependence. Saving faith is not merely believing that God exists. <ScriptureText text="James 2:19" /> warns that even demons believe in God\rquote s existence\emdash and tremble. Therefore, saving faith must go beyond intellectual agreement.</p>\par
        <p>Historically and biblically, saving faith includes three essential dimensions:</p>\par
        <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">\par
            <li><strong>Knowledge (Notitia):</strong> There must be knowledge of the gospel. <ScriptureText text="Romans 10:14" /> asks, \ldblquote How are they to believe in Him of whom they have never heard?\rdblquote  Faith requires content. One must know: Who Jesus is, What He accomplished on the cross, Why His work is necessary. Faith is not blind optimism. It is informed trust grounded in revealed truth (<ScriptureText text="John 20:31" />).</li>\par
            <li><strong>Assent (Assensus):</strong> Assent means agreement that the gospel is true. One may understand facts about Christ but reject them. Saving faith requires acknowledging the truthfulness of: Christ\rquote s deity (<ScriptureText text="John 1:1, 14" />), His atoning death (<ScriptureText text="Isaiah 53:5\endash 6" />), His resurrection (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 15:3\endash 4" />). However, knowledge plus agreement still does not equal saving faith.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Personal Trust (Fiducia):</strong> This is the heart of saving faith. It is not merely believing that Christ saves\emdash it is entrusting oneself to Him. Paul describes this in <ScriptureText text="Philippians 3:8\endash 9" /> as abandoning his own righteousness and depending entirely on Christ. Saving faith involves personal reliance. It says: \ldblquote I cannot save myself. I depend fully on Christ.\rdblquote  Faith transfers trust from self to Savior.</li>\par
        </ol>\par
\par
        <p><strong>III. THE OBJECT OF SAVING FAITH</strong></p>\par
        <p>Faith is only as valid as its object. Saving faith is not faith in: A church, A pastor, A religious system, One\rquote s own goodness. Saving faith is faith in the person and work of Jesus Christ. <ScriptureText text="Acts 4:12" /> declares that there is no other name under heaven given among men by which we must be saved. True faith rests upon: Who Christ is (His person), What Christ has done (His finished work), What Christ promises (eternal life). Faith misplaced is ineffective. Faith placed in Christ saves.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>IV. LORDSHIP OF CHRIST</strong></p>\par
        <p>A critical component often misunderstood in baptismal preparation is the Lordship of Christ. <ScriptureText text="Romans 10:9" /> declares that salvation involves confessing \ldblquote Jesus as Lord\rdblquote  and believing that God raised Him from the dead. The term \ldblquote Lord\rdblquote  (Kyrios) signifies authority, ownership, and sovereignty. To receive Christ as Savior while rejecting Him as Lord is a contradiction. Saving faith acknowledges: His authority over life, His right to command obedience, His ownership of the believer. Jesus Himself asked in <ScriptureText text="Luke 6:46" />, \ldblquote Why do you call me \lquote Lord, Lord,\rquote  and not do what I tell you?\rdblquote  Saving faith produces submission. It does not mean instant perfection, but it does mean surrender. Faith that refuses Christ\rquote s authority is not biblical faith.</p>\par
\par
        <p><strong>V. CONFESSION AND BELIEF</strong></p>\par
        <p><ScriptureText text="Romans 10:9\endash 10" /> teaches that: Belief is in the heart. Confession is with the mouth. This reflects the internal and external dimensions of faith.</p>\par
        <p><strong>1. Belief in the Heart:</strong> Biblical belief is not emotional enthusiasm. It is conviction rooted in trust. Abraham \ldblquote believed God\rdblquote  (<ScriptureText text="Romans 4:3" />), meaning he trusted God\rquote s promise despite circumstances. True belief involves reliance upon Christ\rquote s sufficiency.</p>\par
        <p><strong>2. Confession with the Mouth:</strong> Confession is public identification. In the early church, confessing Jesus as Lord could lead to persecution. Therefore, confession was not casual\emdash it was costly. Jesus declared in <ScriptureText text="Matthew 10:32\endash 33" /> that whoever confesses Him before men, He will confess before the Father. Confession demonstrates authenticity of faith. Silent belief that refuses identification may indicate incomplete surrender. Baptism itself becomes a visible confession.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>VI. THE RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN FAITH AND WORKS</strong></p>\par
        <p>A vital doctrinal balance must be maintained. <ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:8\endash 9" /> clearly teaches salvation is not by works. However, <ScriptureText text="James 2:17" /> teaches that faith without works is dead. The proper order is: Faith \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Justification \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Transformation \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Good Works. Works are evidence of genuine faith, not the cause of salvation. True faith produces obedience (<ScriptureText text="Romans 1:5" /> speaks of \f1\ldblquote\f0 the obedience of faith\f1\rdblquote\f0 ). If there is no transformation, the authenticity of faith must be examined (<ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 13:5" />).</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>VII. ASSURANCE OF SALVATION</strong></p>\par
        <p>Baptismal candidates must understand how assurance works. Assurance is not based on emotion. It is grounded in: The promises of God, The finished work of Christ, The internal witness of the Spirit, The evidence of transformation.</p>\par
        <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">\par
          <li><strong>Assurance Based on God\f1\rquote\f0 s Promise:</strong> <ScriptureText text="1 John 5:11\f1\endash 13" /> states that eternal life is given in the Son, and those who believe may know that they have eternal life. Assurance rests on God\rquote s faithfulness, not personal feelings.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Assurance Based on Christ\rquote s Finished Work:</strong> <ScriptureText text="Hebrews 10:14" /> teaches that by one offering Christ perfected forever those who are being sanctified. If salvation depended on human consistency, no one would be secure. Assurance is anchored in Christ\rquote s sufficiency.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Assurance Through the Spirit\rquote s Witness:</strong> <ScriptureText text="Romans 8:16" /> says the Spirit bears witness with our spirit that we are children of God. The Holy Spirit produces internal confirmation\emdash not mystical voices, but deep conviction of belonging to God.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Assurance Through Transformed Life:</strong> <ScriptureText text="1 John 2:3" /> teaches that we know we have come to know Him if we keep His commandments. Obedience does not create salvation\emdash but it confirms it. A changed life strengthens assurance.</li>\par
        </ol>\par
\par
        <p><strong>VIII. FALSE FAITH VS GENUINE FAITH</strong></p>\par
        <p>Not all belief saves. In <ScriptureText text="John 2:23\endash 25" />, many believed in Jesus, but He did not entrust Himself to them. Their belief was superficial. Temporary faith is described in the parable of the sower (<ScriptureText text="Luke 8:13" />)\emdash they believe for a while but fall away under trial. Genuine faith perseveres because it is rooted in regeneration.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>CONCLUSION FOR BAPTISMAL CANDIDATES</strong></p>\par
        <p>Before baptism, a candidate should be able to affirm: I understand the gospel. I believe Jesus is the Son of God who died and rose again. I have placed my trust fully in Him. I receive Him as Lord, not only Savior. I rest in His promise for eternal life. Saving faith is wholehearted trust in the crucified and risen Christ, resulting in surrender, transformation, and confident assurance.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
  \{\par
    id: 4,\par
    title: 'LESSON 4: THE NEW BIRTH (REGENERATION)',\par
    description: 'Understanding why the new birth is necessary and what it means to be born again.',\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
        <p><strong>I. WHY THE NEW BIRTH IS NECESSARY</strong></p>\par
        <p>When Nicodemus came to Jesus at night (<ScriptureText text="John 3:1\endash 10" />), he was not an immoral pagan. He was a Pharisee, a ruler of the Jews, a teacher of Israel. Yet Jesus did not commend his morality or religion. Instead, He made a startling declaration: \ldblquote Unless one is born again he cannot see the kingdom of God\rdblquote  (<ScriptureText text="John 3:3" />). Jesus repeats it in <ScriptureText text="John 3:5" /> \emdash  \ldblquote Unless one is born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God.\rdblquote </p>\par
        <p>Notice the absolute language: Not \ldblquote should.\rdblquote  Not \ldblquote it is advisable.\rdblquote  But \ldblquote unless.\rdblquote  This tells us something foundational: Religion cannot replace regeneration. Morality cannot replace new birth. Knowledge cannot replace spiritual life. Why? Because humanity\rquote s problem is not merely behavioral \emdash  it is spiritual death. <ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:1" /> describes humanity as \ldblquote dead in trespasses and sins.\rdblquote  A dead person does not need improvement; he needs life. Therefore, regeneration is necessary because: Man is spiritually dead. Man cannot reform himself into spiritual life. Only God can impart new life.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>II. WHAT IT MEANS TO BE BORN AGAIN</strong></p>\par
        <p>The phrase \ldblquote born again\rdblquote  in <ScriptureText text="John 3:3" /> can also mean \ldblquote born from above.\rdblquote  Both meanings are theologically significant. Regeneration is: A supernatural act of God. Where spiritual life is imparted to a spiritually dead sinner. Resulting in a new spiritual identity and nature. It is not: Joining a church. Becoming religious. Emotional excitement. Water baptism. Regeneration is a divine work.</p>\par
        <p><ScriptureText text="James 1:18" /> says, \ldblquote Of His own will He brought us forth by the word of truth.\rdblquote  <ScriptureText text="1 Peter 1:23" /> says we are \ldblquote\f0 born again\f1\'85 \f0 through the living and abiding word of God.\f1\rdblquote  Notice two elements: The Spirit of God (<ScriptureText text="John 3:5\endash 8" />). The Word of God (<ScriptureText text="1 Peter 1:23" />). Regeneration is initiated by God, not by human effort (<ScriptureText text="John 1:12\endash 13" />). <ScriptureText text="John 1:13" /> makes this explicit: \ldblquote Who were born, not of blood nor of the will of the flesh nor of the will of man, but of God.\rdblquote  New birth is not inherited. It is not self-generated. It is not conferred by another human being. It is an act of God.</p>\par
\par
        <p><strong>III. THE NATURE OF REGENERATION</strong></p>\par
        <p>Regeneration is not symbolic. It is ontological \emdash  it changes the inner being. <ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 5:17" /> declares: \ldblquote If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation; the old has passed away; behold, the new has come.\rdblquote  Notice the language: Not improved creation. Not repaired creation. New creation. <ScriptureText text="Ezekiel 36:26\endash 27" /> prophesied this: \ldblquote\f0 I will give you a new heart\f1\'85 \f0 and I will put my Spirit within you.\f1\rdblquote  Regeneration involves: A new heart. A new spirit. A new disposition. A new capacity for obedience. It is not external adjustment. It is internal transformation.</p>\par
\par
        <p><strong>IV. OLD NATURE VS NEW NATURE</strong></p>\par
        <p>Understanding this prevents confusion in Christian growth.</p>\par
        <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">\par
            <li><strong>The Old Nature:</strong> The old nature refers to our fallen identity in Adam. <ScriptureText text="Romans 5:12" /> explains that sin entered through one man and death through sin. The old nature is characterized by: Hostility toward God (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:7" />), Inability to please God (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:8" />), Slavery to sin (<ScriptureText text="John 8:34" />), Darkened understanding (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 4:18" />). This nature does not submit to God because it cannot.</li>\par
            <li><strong>The New Nature:</strong> Through regeneration, the believer receives new spiritual life. <ScriptureText text="2 Peter 1:4" /> speaks of becoming \ldblquote partakers of the divine nature.\rdblquote  This does not mean becoming divine, but sharing in a renewed moral disposition aligned with God. The new nature: Desires righteousness, Loves God, Hates sin, Responds to God\rquote s Word. <ScriptureText text="1 John 3:9" /> states that one born of God does not continue in habitual, unbroken sin because God\rquote s seed abides in him. This does not teach sinless perfection \emdash  but it does teach transformation.</li>\par
            <li><strong>The Ongoing Conflict:</strong> Even after regeneration, believers experience internal struggle. <ScriptureText text="Galatians 5:16\endash 17" /> explains the conflict between flesh and Spirit. The presence of struggle does not mean absence of regeneration. It often confirms it. Before regeneration, there was comfort in sin. After regeneration, there is conflict with sin.</li>\par
        </ol>\par
\par
        <p><strong>V. EVIDENCE OF NEW LIFE</strong></p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
          <li><strong>New Affection for God:</strong> <ScriptureText text="1 John 4:7" /> says everyone who loves has been born of God. Love for God and His people is a sign of new birth.</li>\par
          <li><strong>New Relationship to Sin:</strong> <ScriptureText text="1 John 3:9" /> teaches that the one born of God does not live in continuous, unrepentant sin. The believer may stumble, but does not settle.</li>\par
          <li><strong>New Desire for Righteousness:</strong> <ScriptureText text="Matthew 5:6" /> \emdash  hunger and thirst for righteousness. A regenerated heart craves holiness.</li>\par
          <li><strong>New Response to God\rquote s Word:</strong> <ScriptureText text="1 Peter 2:2" /> compares believers to newborn infants longing for pure spiritual milk. A desire for Scripture signals spiritual life.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Perseverance in Faith:</strong> <ScriptureText text="1 John 2:19" /> teaches that true believers remain. Regeneration produces endurance.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p><strong>VI. THE INDWELLING HOLY SPIRIT</strong></p>\par
        <p>Regeneration and indwelling are inseparable. <ScriptureText text="Romans 8:9" /> states clearly: \ldblquote Anyone who does not have the Spirit of Christ does not belong to Him.\rdblquote  At conversion: The Spirit regenerates, The Spirit indwells, The Spirit seals (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 1:13\endash 14" />). The Spirit\rquote s ministry includes: Giving life (<ScriptureText text="John 6:63" />), Testifying to our sonship (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:16" />), Producing fruit (<ScriptureText text="Galatians 5:22\endash 23" />), Empowering obedience (<ScriptureText text="Ezekiel 36:27" />). The Christian life is not self-powered morality. It is Spirit-enabled transformation.</p>\par
\par
        <p><strong>VII. REGENERATION AND BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
        <p>Baptism symbolizes what regeneration accomplishes. <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:3\endash 4" /> describes burial and resurrection imagery. However: Water does not regenerate. The Spirit regenerates. <ScriptureText text="Titus 3:5" /> states that salvation comes through \ldblquote the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Holy Spirit.\rdblquote  Baptism testifies outwardly to inward new birth.</p>\par
\par
        <p><strong>VIII. COMMON MISUNDERSTANDINGS</strong></p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
          <li>\ldblquote I was born in a Christian family, so I am born again.\rdblquote  \endash  <ScriptureText text="John 1:13" /> denies inherited regeneration.</li>\par
          <li>\ldblquote I was baptized as a child, so I am born again.\rdblquote  \endash  Regeneration is a spiritual act, not a ritual.</li>\par
          <li>\ldblquote I prayed a prayer once.\rdblquote  \endash  Regeneration produces ongoing transformation.</li>\par
          <li>\ldblquote I feel different.\rdblquote  \endash  Feelings may accompany it, but regeneration is not emotionalism.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
\par
        <p><strong>CONCLUSION FOR BAPTISMAL CANDIDATES</strong></p>\par
        <p>A person preparing for baptism must examine: Has my heart changed? Do I love what I once hated? Do I hate what I once loved? Is there evidence of spiritual life? Does the Spirit bear witness within me? The new birth is not cosmetic change. It is spiritual resurrection. Just as physical birth introduces a person into physical life, Regeneration introduces a person into spiritual life.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
  \{\par
    id: 5,\par
    title: 'LESSON 5: WHAT IS BAPTISM?',\par
    description: 'Defining baptism, its symbolism, and its role as a public declaration of faith.',\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
        <p><strong>I. THE DEFINITION OF BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
        <p>The New Testament word translated \ldblquote baptize\rdblquote  is baptiz\f4\u333?, which fundamentally means: To immerse, To dip, To plunge, To submerge. It was used in secular Greek literature for: Dipping cloth into dye, Submerging objects in liquid, Immersing something completely. The word itself implies full immersion, not sprinkling. This understanding is consistent with the description of Jesus\f1\rquote  \f4 baptism in <ScriptureText text="Matthew 3:16" />, where He \f1\ldblquote\f4 came up immediately from the water,\f1\rdblquote  \f4 implying prior descent into it. Likewise, in <ScriptureText text="Acts 8:38\f1\endash 39" />, both Philip and the Ethiopian eunuch \ldblquote went down into the water\rdblquote  and \ldblquote came up out of the water.\rdblquote  Therefore, biblically defined, baptism is immersion in water. However, the meaning is deeper than the method.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>II. WHAT BAPTISM IS \emdash  AND WHAT IT IS NOT</strong></p>\par
        <p>Before defining what baptism is, we must clarify what it is not. Baptism is not: The act that regenerates the sinner. The means by which sins are automatically removed. A substitute for repentance and faith. A ritual that works apart from belief. Regeneration occurs by the Spirit (<ScriptureText text="John 3:5\endash 8; Titus 3:5" />). Justification occurs through faith (<ScriptureText text="Romans 5:1" />). Therefore, baptism does not cause salvation; it follows salvation. Baptism belongs to those who have already repented, believed, and been born again.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>III. THE SYMBOLISM OF WATER BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
        <p>Baptism is a visible sign of an invisible reality. The clearest theological explanation appears in <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:3\endash 4" />. Paul writes that those baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into His death, buried with Him, and raised to walk in newness of life. Baptism symbolizes three realities:</p>\par
        <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">\par
            <li><strong>Death to the Old Life:</strong> Going under the water represents death. It symbolizes: Death to sin (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:6\endash 7" />), Death to the old self, Death to the dominion of sin. This is not symbolic death alone \emdash  it reflects a spiritual reality that occurred in regeneration.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Burial of the Old Man:</strong> Burial confirms death. <ScriptureText text="Colossians 2:12" /> speaks of being \ldblquote buried with Him in baptism.\rdblquote  Burial signifies: Final separation from the former life, The end of old identity in Adam, Public acknowledgment that the old life is gone.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Resurrection to New Life:</strong> Coming up out of the water symbolizes resurrection. <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:4" /> declares that just as Christ was raised from the dead, we too walk in newness of life. Baptism proclaims: I have been raised spiritually, I now live under Christ\rquote s lordship, I belong to the risen Savior. Thus baptism is a gospel drama \emdash  enacted physically, declaring spiritual reality.</li>\par
        </ol>\par
\par
        <p><strong>IV. BAPTISM AS A PUBLIC DECLARATION OF FAITH</strong></p>\par
        <p>Salvation is personal, but it is never private. In <ScriptureText text="Romans 10:9\endash 10" />, belief is in the heart, but confession is with the mouth. Baptism becomes the embodied confession of faith. In the early church, baptism was immediate and public (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:41; Acts 8:12; Acts 16:31\endash 33" />). There was no concept of secret discipleship. Jesus declared in <ScriptureText text="Matthew 10:32" /> that whoever acknowledges Him before men, He will acknowledge before the Father. Baptism declares publicly: I belong to Christ, I am no longer ashamed of the gospel, My allegiance has changed. In many contexts historically, baptism marked a decisive break with former religious identity. It was a line of separation. Therefore, baptism is covenantal identification.</p>\par
\par
        <p><strong>V. IDENTIFICATION WITH CHRIST</strong></p>\par
        <p>The deepest meaning of baptism is union with Christ. The believer is united to Christ in: His death, His burial, His resurrection. <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:5" /> says, \ldblquote If we have been united with Him in a death like His, we shall certainly be united with Him in a resurrection like His.\rdblquote  Baptism does not create that union \emdash  faith does. But baptism testifies to that union. <ScriptureText text="Galatians 3:27" /> says, \ldblquote As many of you as were baptized into Christ have put on Christ.\rdblquote  This language reflects identification \emdash  like putting on a garment. The believer now bears the identity of Christ.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>VI. BAPTISM AND DISCIPLESHIP</strong></p>\par
        <p>In the Great Commission (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:19\endash 20" />), Jesus commands the church to: Make disciples, Baptize them, Teach them to observe all He commanded. Notice the sequence: Disciples are made, Disciples are baptized, Disciples are taught. Baptism marks entry into visible discipleship. It is not the end of conversion \emdash  it is the beginning of obedient living.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>VII. THE SPIRITUAL SERIOUSNESS OF BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
        <p>Baptism is simple, but it is not casual. It signifies: Allegiance, Submission, Union, Covenant commitment. It says: \ldblquote I am no longer my own.\rdblquote  In <ScriptureText text="1 Peter 3:21" />, baptism is described as an appeal to God for a good conscience \emdash  not the removal of dirt from the body, but a pledge flowing from inward transformation. Therefore, baptism must never be reduced to ceremony.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>VIII. WHO SHOULD BE BAPTIZED?</strong></p>\par
        <p>From the New Testament pattern: Those who repent (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38" />), Those who believe (<ScriptureText text="Acts 8:12" />), Those who receive the word (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:41" />). There is no example of unrepentant or unbelieving individuals being baptized. Therefore, baptism is for regenerated believers.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>CONCLUSION FOR BAPTISMAL CANDIDATES</strong></p>\par
        <p>Before baptism, each candidate should understand: Baptism does not save me. I have already repented and believed. I have been born again. I am publicly declaring my union with Christ. I am identifying with His death and resurrection. I am submitting to His Lordship. Baptism is the outward sign of inward transformation and covenant allegiance to Jesus Christ.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
  \{\par
    id: 6,\par
    title: 'LESSON 6: BIBLICAL FOUNDATION OF BAPTISM',\par
    description: 'Exploring the biblical command, apostolic practice, and doctrinal teaching of baptism.',\par
    content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
        <p><strong>I. BAPTISM COMMANDED BY JESUS (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:19\endash 20" />)</strong></p>\par
        <p>After His resurrection and before His ascension, Jesus gave what is commonly called the Great Commission in <ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:19\endash 20" />. Several foundational truths emerge from this command:</p>\par
        <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">\par
          <li><strong>Baptism is not optional:</strong> It is a command from the risen Christ who possesses \ldblquote all authority in heaven and on earth\rdblquote  (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:18" />). To refuse baptism knowingly is to resist a direct command of Christ.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Baptism is connected to discipleship:</strong> The structure of the command shows the process: Make disciples \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Baptize them \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Teach them. Baptism follows discipleship.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Baptism is Trinitarian:</strong> Jesus commands baptism \f1\ldblquote\f0 in the name\f1\rdblquote  \f0 (singular) \f1\ldblquote\f0 of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.\f1\rdblquote  \f0 This shows the unity of the Godhead and the believer\f1\rquote\f0 s entry into covenant relationship with the Triune God.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Baptism belongs to the New Covenant mission:</strong> The command is given after the resurrection, marking the inauguration of the church age.</li>\par
        </ol>\par
\par
        <p><strong>II. BAPTISM PRACTICED IN THE EARLY CHURCH (Acts of the Apostles)</strong></p>\par
        <p>The Book of Acts provides historical evidence that the apostles obeyed Christ\f1\rquote\f0 s command immediately and consistently. Baptism followed conversion closely.</p>\par
        <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">\par
          <li><strong>On the Day of Pentecost (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38\f1\endash 41" />):</strong> Those who received his word were baptized. Three thousand were baptized the same day.</li>\par
          <li><strong>In Samaria (<ScriptureText text="Acts 8:12" />):</strong> When they believed Philip, \ldblquote they were baptized, both men and women.\rdblquote </li>\par
          <li><strong>The Ethiopian Eunuch (<ScriptureText text="Acts 8:35\endash 38" />):</strong> He was baptized upon confession of faith.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Saul of Tarsus (<ScriptureText text="Acts 9:18; 22:16" />):</strong> After encountering Christ, he was baptized.</li>\par
          <li><strong>Cornelius\rquote  Household (<ScriptureText text="Acts 10:44\endash 48" />):</strong> The Spirit fell upon them, then Peter commanded them to be baptized.</li>\par
          <li><strong>The Philippian Jailer (<ScriptureText text="Acts 16:30\endash 33" />):</strong> He and his household were baptized that same night.</li>\par
        </ul>\par
        <p><strong>Observations from Acts:</strong> Across all accounts, baptism follows repentance and faith and is administered immediately. There is no example of baptism without belief.</p>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>III. THE DOCTRINAL TEACHING OF PAUL (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:1\endash 6" />)</strong></p>\par
        <p>While Acts shows practice, Paul provides theological explanation. In <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:3\endash 4" />, Paul clarifies the meaning of baptism:</p>\par
        <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">\par
            <li><strong>Baptism signifies union with Christ:</strong> Being \ldblquote baptized into Christ\rdblquote  reflects spiritual union.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Baptism signifies death to sin:</strong> <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:6" /> states that our old self was crucified with Him, breaking sin\rquote s dominion.</li>\par
            <li><strong>Baptism signifies resurrection life:</strong> <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:4" /> declares we are raised \ldblquote to walk in newness of life.\rdblquote </li>\par
            <li><strong>Paul assumes baptism for all believers:</strong> Paul assumes every Christian in Rome had been baptized. There was no category of unbaptized believer living indefinitely outside obedience.</li>\par
        </ol>\par
        \par
        <p><strong>IV. SUMMARY: THE THREEFOLD FOUNDATION</strong></p>\par
        <p>Baptism rests on three pillars: Commanded by Christ, Practiced by the apostles, Explained doctrinally by Paul. Therefore, baptism is Christ-ordained, apostolically practiced, and theologically grounded. It is not denominational tradition. It is New Testament Christianity.</p>\par
\par
        <p><strong>CONCLUSION FOR CANDIDATES</strong></p>\par
        <p>To reject baptism is to: Ignore Christ\rquote s command, Depart from apostolic pattern, Dismiss apostolic teaching. To receive baptism is to: Obey Christ, Follow the early church, Publicly affirm union with Him.</p>\par
      </div>\par
    )\par
  \},\par
  \{\par
    id: 7,\par
    title: 'LESSON 7: WHY BAPTISM IS IMPORTANT & THE MODE AND FORMULA',\par
    description: 'Understanding the importance, biblical mode, and the formula of baptism.',\par
    content: (\par
        <div className="space-y-4">\par
            <p><strong>PART I \emdash  WHY BAPTISM IS IMPORTANT</strong></p>\par
            <p>Baptism is not a minor Christian activity. It is foundational obedience.</p>\par
            \par
            <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-4">\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>Baptism as an Act of Obedience</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p>The primary reason baptism is important is simple: Christ commanded it (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:19\endash 20" />).</p>\par
                        <p>Obedience is the first evidence of genuine faith. Jesus said in <ScriptureText text="John 14:15" />, \ldblquote If you love me, you will keep my commandments.\rdblquote  Baptism becomes the believer\rquote s first public act of obedience.</p>\par
                        <p>In <ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38" />, Peter commands, \ldblquote\f0 Repent and be baptized\f1\'85\rdblquote  It was not presented as optional.</p>\par
                        <p>Deliberate refusal to be baptized, when one understands Christ\rquote s command, reveals either:</p>\par
                        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                            <li>Ignorance,</li>\par
                            <li>Fear of public identification,</li>\par
                            <li>Or resistance to Christ\rquote s authority.</li>\par
                        </ul>\par
                        <p>True disciples obey.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>Baptism as Public Testimony</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p>Christianity is not a secret faith.</p>\par
                        <p><ScriptureText text="Romans 10:9\endash 10" /> teaches that belief in the heart results in confession with the mouth. Baptism becomes the visible confession.</p>\par
                        <p>In <ScriptureText text="Acts 8:36\endash 38" />, the Ethiopian eunuch publicly identified with Christ through baptism. In <ScriptureText text="Acts 16:33" />, the Philippian jailer was baptized openly.</p>\par
                        <p>Baptism testifies:</p>\par
                        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                            <li>I have repented.</li>\par
                            <li>I believe in Jesus Christ.</li>\par
                            <li>I now belong to Him.</li>\par
                        </ul>\par
                        <p>It marks a transfer of allegiance (<ScriptureText text="Colossians 1:13" />).</p>\par
                        <p>In many cultures, baptism historically marked the decisive break from former religious identity.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>Identification with Christ\rquote s Death and Resurrection</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p><ScriptureText text="Romans 6:3\endash 4" /> explains that those baptized into Christ are baptized into His death, buried with Him, and raised to walk in newness of life.</p>\par
                        <p>Baptism dramatizes union with Christ.</p>\par
                        <p>Through faith:</p>\par
                        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                            <li>We died with Him (<ScriptureText text="Galatians 2:20" />).</li>\par
                            <li>We were buried with Him (<ScriptureText text="Colossians 2:12" />).</li>\par
                            <li>We were raised with Him (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:5\endash 6" />).</li>\par
                        </ul>\par
                        <p>Baptism visibly proclaims that reality.</p>\par
                        <p>It declares: \ldblquote My old life ended at the cross.\rdblquote </p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>Spiritual Significance \emdash  Burial of the Old Life</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p>Burial signifies finality.</p>\par
                        <p><ScriptureText text="Romans 6:6" /> says the old self was crucified with Him so that we would no longer be enslaved to sin.</p>\par
                        <p>Going under the water represents:</p>\par
                        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                            <li>Death to the dominion of sin.</li>\par
                            <li>The burial of the old identity in Adam.</li>\par
                            <li>The renunciation of the former way of life.</li>\par
                        </ul>\par
                        <p>Coming out of the water represents:</p>\par
                        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                            <li>Resurrection life.</li>\par
                            <li>New identity in Christ.</li>\par
                            <li>A new walk empowered by the Spirit.</li>\par
                        </ul>\par
                        <p>While the water does not regenerate, baptism carries deep spiritual meaning because it declares what God has already done internally.</p>\par
                        <p>It is covenantal symbolism.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
            </ol>\par
\par
            <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
            <p><strong>PART II \emdash  THE MODE OF BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
            <p className="italic text-sm text-gray-600">(Immersion vs Sprinkling)</p>\par
            <p>This must be addressed biblically and historically.</p>\par
\par
            <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-4">\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>The Meaning of Baptiz\f4\u333?</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-1">\par
                        <p>The Greek word <em>baptiz\u333?</em> means:</p>\par
                        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                            <li>To immerse,</li>\par
                            <li>To dip,</li>\par
                            <li>To plunge.</li>\par
                        </ul>\par
                        <p>There is no lexical evidence that it means sprinkling.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>Biblical Descriptions Suggest Immersion</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p><ScriptureText text="Matthew 3:16" /> says Jesus came up \f1\ldblquote\f4 out of the water.\f1\rdblquote\f4 </p>\par
                        <p><ScriptureText text="John 3:23" /> says John baptized where there was \f1\ldblquote\f4 much water.\f1\rdblquote\f4 </p>\par
                        <p><ScriptureText text="Acts 8:38\f1\endash 39" /> says Philip and the eunuch went down into the water and came up out of the water.</p>\par
                        <p>The language suggests immersion.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>The Symbolism Requires Immersion</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p><ScriptureText text="Romans 6:4" /> speaks of burial and resurrection.</p>\par
                        <p>Sprinkling does not symbolize burial.</p>\par
                        <p>Immersion best portrays:</p>\par
                        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                            <li>Death,</li>\par
                            <li>Burial,</li>\par
                            <li>Resurrection.</li>\par
                        </ul>\par
                        <p>Therefore, immersion most fully reflects the biblical imagery.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>Historical Consideration</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2">\par
                        <p>Early Christian writings (first and second centuries) indicate immersion as the normal mode, though allowances were sometimes made for illness or exceptional cases. The dominant apostolic practice was immersion.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
            </ol>\par
\par
            <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
            <p><strong>THE FORMULA OF BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
            <p className="italic text-sm text-gray-600">(In the Name of Jesus vs Trinitarian Formula)</p>\par
\par
            <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-4">\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>The Command of Christ</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p><ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:19" /> commands baptism \ldblquote in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.\rdblquote </p>\par
                        <p>This is explicit and direct from Jesus. The singular \ldblquote name\rdblquote  indicates unity; the three Persons reveal the Trinity. This formula reflects Trinitarian theology.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>Baptism \ldblquote In the Name of Jesus\rdblquote  in Acts</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p>In <ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38; 8:16; 10:48; 19:5" />, baptism is described as being done \ldblquote in the name of Jesus Christ\rdblquote  or \ldblquote in the name of the Lord Jesus.\rdblquote </p>\par
                        <p>However, in Acts, the phrase emphasizes:</p>\par
                        <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                            <li>Authority and Allegiance.</li>\par
                            <li>Distinction from Jewish or pagan baptisms.</li>\par
                            <li>Identification with Jesus as Messiah and Lord.</li>\par
                        </ul>\par
                        <p>It does not necessarily record the exact spoken formula, but rather the authority under which baptism occurred.</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
\par
                <li>\par
                    <strong>Harmonizing the Two</strong>\par
                    <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                        <p>There is no contradiction. Matthew gives the baptismal command. Acts emphasizes allegiance to Jesus as Lord.</p>\par
                        <p>To be baptized in the name of the Trinity is to be baptized under Christ\rquote s authority, since Christ reveals the Father and sends the Spirit (<ScriptureText text="John 14:16\endash 17; John 14:26" />).</p>\par
                    </div>\par
                </li>\par
            </ol>\par
\par
            <p><strong>THEOLOGICAL CLARITY</strong></p>\par
            <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                <li>Trinitarian in nature.</li>\par
                <li>Christ-centered in authority.</li>\par
                <li>Spirit-empowered in meaning.</li>\par
            </ul>\par
            <p>It signifies entry into relationship with the Triune God through union with Christ by the Spirit.</p>\par
\par
            <p><strong>CONCLUSION</strong></p>\par
            <p>Baptism is important because Christ commanded it, the apostles practiced it, and it symbolizes the burial of the old life. It is administered by immersion because the word requires it and the symbolism demands it. It is done in the name of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, under the authority of Jesus Christ.</p>\par
        </div>\par
    )\par
\},\par
\{\par
  id: 8,\par
  title: 'LESSON 8: PERTINENT QUESTIONS ABOUT WATER BAPTISM',\par
  description: 'Addressing common misconceptions, the proper biblical understanding, and practical questions regarding baptism.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p><strong>PART I \emdash  WHAT BAPTISM IS NOT</strong></p>\par
          <p>Clarity prevents error. Let us begin by removing common misconceptions.</p>\par
\par
          <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-4">\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Baptism Is NOT Salvation Itself</strong>\par
                  <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                      <p>Salvation is by grace through faith (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:8\endash 9" />), and justification is by faith apart from works (<ScriptureText text="Romans 3:28; Romans 5:1" />).</p>\par
                      <p>The thief on the cross was promised paradise without baptism (<ScriptureText text="Luke 23:42\endash 43" />).</p>\par
                      <p>If baptism itself saved, Paul could not have said, \ldblquote Christ did not send me to baptize but to preach the gospel\rdblquote  (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 1:17" />).</p>\par
                      <p>Baptism follows salvation; it does not produce it. Faith unites the believer to Christ (<ScriptureText text="John 1:12; Galatians 2:20" />). Baptism testifies to that union.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </li>\par
\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Baptism Is NOT the Removal of Sin</strong>\par
                  <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                      <p>Sin is removed by the blood of Christ (<ScriptureText text="1 John 1:7; Revelation 1:5" />). Forgiveness comes through repentance and faith (<ScriptureText text="Acts 10:43" />).</p>\par
                      <p><ScriptureText text="1 Peter 3:21" /> explicitly clarifies that baptism is \ldblquote not the removal of dirt from the body,\rdblquote  but an appeal to God for a good conscience through the resurrection of Jesus Christ.</p>\par
                      <p>Water cleans the body; only Christ cleanses the conscience (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 9:14" />).</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </li>\par
\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Baptism Is NOT Church Membership by Itself</strong>\par
                  <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                      <p>While baptism publicly identifies a believer with the visible church (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:41\endash 42" />), it does not automatically guarantee spiritual life.</p>\par
                      <p>A person may be baptized and yet remain unregenerate if there was no genuine repentance or faith (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 7:21\endash 23" />).</p>\par
                      <p>The church receives baptized believers, but the new birth is the true entrance into the body of Christ (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 12:13" />).</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </li>\par
\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Baptism Is NOT a Magical Ritual</strong>\par
                  <div className="mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                      <p>The water has no mystical power. There is no spiritual transformation in the chemical properties of water.</p>\par
                      <p>The power of salvation lies in the gospel (<ScriptureText text="Romans 1:16" />), not in the act itself. When baptism is treated as mechanically effective, it becomes ritualism instead of obedience flowing from faith.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </li>\par
          </ol>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>PART II \emdash  WATER BAPTISM PROPERLY UNDERSTOOD</strong></p>\par
          \par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold">1. The Mode: Immersion vs Sprinkling</p>\par
                  <ul className="list-disc pl-5 mt-2 space-y-2">\par
                      <li><strong>Lexical Meaning:</strong> The Greek word <em>baptiz\f4\u333?</em> means to immerse or submerge. It was used for dipping garments into dye. There is no linguistic evidence that it means sprinkle.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Biblical Descriptions:</strong> Jesus came up \f1\ldblquote\f4 out of the water\f1\rdblquote  \f4 (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 3:16" />). John baptized where there was \f1\ldblquote\f4 much water\f1\rdblquote  \f4 (<ScriptureText text="John 3:23" />). Philip and the eunuch went down into the water and came up out of the water (<ScriptureText text="Acts 8:38\f1\endash 39" />).</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Theological Symbolism:</strong> <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:4" /> and <ScriptureText text="Colossians 2:12" /> describe being \ldblquote buried with him.\rdblquote  Burial requires immersion imagery; sprinkling does not convey this.</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold">2. Who Should Be Baptized?</p>\par
                  <p>The New Testament pattern is consistent: Faith precedes baptism.</p>\par
                  <ul className="list-disc pl-5 mt-2">\par
                      <li>Those Who Repent (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38" />)</li>\par
                      <li>Those Who Believe (<ScriptureText text="Acts 8:12; Acts 18:8" />)</li>\par
                      <li>Those Who Receive the Word (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:41" />)</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold">3. When Should One Be Baptized?</p>\par
                  <p>The New Testament shows immediacy: the 3,000 on Pentecost (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:41" />), the Eunuch (<ScriptureText text="Acts 8:36\endash 38" />), and the Jailer (<ScriptureText text="Acts 16:33" />). Baptism should follow genuine understanding and a credible profession of faith.</p>\par
              </section>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>PART III \emdash  OTHER IMPORTANT QUESTIONS</strong></p>\par
\par
          <ul className="space-y-4">\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Should Someone Be Re-baptized?</strong>\par
                  <p>If the first act preceded genuine faith, it was not true believer's baptism. <ScriptureText text="Acts 19:3\endash 5" /> shows re-baptism is appropriate when there is new, genuine faith. If the first was biblical and followed faith, repetition is unnecessary (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 4:5" />).</p>\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>What If Someone Was Sprinkled as an Infant?</strong>\par
                  <p>Since infants cannot repent or believe (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38; Acts 8:12" />), believer's baptism upon personal faith is necessary to follow the New Testament model.</p>\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Is Baptism Necessary for Salvation?</strong>\par
                  <p>Necessary for obedience, not justification. Salvation rests on Christ\rquote s finished work (<ScriptureText text="John 19:30" />), received by faith. However, willful refusal raises questions about submission to Christ\rquote s lordship (<ScriptureText text="Luke 6:46" />).</p>\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>What About Acts 2:38 \emdash  \ldblquote For the Forgiveness of Sins\rdblquote ?</strong>\par
                  <p>Forgiveness comes through faith (<ScriptureText text="Acts 10:43" />). In <ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38" />, Peter calls believers to identify with the One who forgives; he is not contradicting justification by faith.</p>\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>What If Someone Is Afraid of Public Baptism?</strong>\par
                  <p>Jesus warned about being ashamed of Him (<ScriptureText text="Mark 8:38" />). Baptism is often the first decisive public stand for Christ; fear must yield to obedience.</p>\par
              </li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-6">\par
              <p><strong>FINAL SUMMARY</strong></p>\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-2 text-sm">\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="font-semibold text-red-600 underline">What it is NOT:</p>\par
                      <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                          <li>Does not save.</li>\par
                          <li>Does not remove sin.</li>\par
                          <li>Does not magically transform.</li>\par
                      </ul>\par
                  </div>\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="font-semibold text-green-600 underline">What it IS:</p>\par
                      <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                          <li>Commanded by Christ.</li>\par
                          <li>Follows conversion.</li>\par
                          <li>Symbolizes death and resurrection.</li>\par
                          <li>Public identification with Christ.</li>\par
                      </ul>\par
                  </div>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\{\par
  id: 9,\par
  title: 'LESSON 9: THE RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN WATER BAPTISM AND THE BAPTISM OF THE HOLY SPIRIT',\par
  description: 'Understanding the distinction and relationship between external water baptism and internal Spirit baptism.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p><strong>I. WHAT IS THE BAPTISM OF THE HOLY SPIRIT?</strong></p>\par
          <p>The baptism of the Holy Spirit is the act by which the risen Christ places the believer into union with Himself and incorporates the believer into His body by the Spirit.</p>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 12:13" /> states: <em>\ldblquote\f0 For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body\f1\'85 \f0 and all were made to drink of one Spirit.\f1\rdblquote </em></p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">\par
              <li>It is Spirit-initiated and universal for believers (\ldblquote we all\rdblquote ).</li>\par
              <li>It unites believers to Christ\rquote s body.</li>\par
              <li>It is not limited to a spiritual elite; it is about spiritual incorporation.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. PROMISE OF THE HOLY SPIRIT</strong></p>\par
          <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-3">\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Promised by John the Baptist:</strong> John said Christ would \ldblquote baptize you with the Holy Spirit\rdblquote  (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 3:11" />).\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Promised by Jesus:</strong> Before His ascension, Jesus told His disciples they would be \ldblquote baptized with the Holy Spirit not many days from now\rdblquote  (<ScriptureText text="Acts 1:5" />) and receive power (<ScriptureText text="Acts 1:8" />).\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Fulfilled at Pentecost:</strong> In <ScriptureText text="Acts 2:1\endash 4" />, the Spirit descended, marking the birth of the church and the fulfillment of Joel\rquote s prophecy (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:16\endash 17" />).\par
              </li>\par
          </ol>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>III. DIFFERENCE BETWEEN WATER BAPTISM AND SPIRIT BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
          <div className="overflow-x-auto">\par
              <table className="min-w-full border-collapse border border-gray-200 text-sm">\par
                  <thead>\par
                      <tr className="bg-gray-100">\par
                          <th className="border p-2 text-left">Feature</th>\par
                          <th className="border p-2 text-left">Water Baptism</th>\par
                          <th className="border p-2 text-left">Spirit Baptism</th>\par
                      </tr>\par
                  </thead>\par
                  <tbody>\par
                      <tr>\par
                          <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Administrator</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Human Servant</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Jesus Christ</td>\par
                      </tr>\par
                      <tr>\par
                          <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Element</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Water</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">The Holy Spirit</td>\par
                      </tr>\par
                      <tr>\par
                          <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Nature</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Symbolic / External</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Spiritual / Internal</td>\par
                      </tr>\par
                      <tr>\par
                          <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Timing</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Post-Conversion</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">At Conversion</td>\par
                      </tr>\par
                  </tbody>\par
              </table>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. INDWELLING AND INFILLING</strong></p>\par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <div>\par
                  <p className="font-bold underline">1. The Indwelling of the Spirit</p>\par
                  <p>At conversion, the Spirit indwells the believer permanently (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:11" />). Our body becomes a temple (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 6:19" />). This is the mark of belonging to Christ (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:9" />).</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div>\par
                  <p className="font-bold underline">2. The Infilling (Filling) of the Spirit</p>\par
                  <p>This refers to ongoing empowerment and control. <ScriptureText text="Ephesians 5:18" /> commands us to \ldblquote be filled\rdblquote  (keep being filled). Unlike baptism, filling is repeatable (<ScriptureText text="Acts 4:31" />) and relates to boldness and holy living.</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>V. EVIDENCE AND MANIFESTATIONS</strong></p>\par
          <p>While manifestations like tongues occurred at transitional moments in Acts (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:4; 10:44\endash 46" />), <ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 12:29\endash 30" /> clarifies that not all speak in tongues. The universal evidence is:</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
              <li>New life (<ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 5:17" />).</li>\par
              <li>Christlike fruit (<ScriptureText text="Galatians 5:22\endash 23" />).</li>\par
              <li>Confessing Christ as Lord (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 12:3" />).</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <p><strong>VI. PURPOSE OF SPIRIT EMPOWERMENT</strong></p>\par
          <p>As per <ScriptureText text="Acts 1:8" />, the Spirit empowers us for <strong>witness, boldness, service,</strong> and <strong>sanctification</strong> (<ScriptureText text="2 Thessalonians 2:13" />).</p>\par
\par
          <p><strong>VII. RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN THE TWO</strong></p>\par
          <p>They are distinct but related: Spirit baptism is the <strong>inward reality</strong>; water baptism is the <strong>outward testimony</strong>. While the order varies in the narrative of Acts, the normative theological pattern is conversion/Spirit baptism followed by water baptism.</p>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-200">\par
              <p className="font-bold mb-2">VIII. ERRORS TO AVOID</p>\par
              <ul className="list-disc pl-5 text-sm space-y-1">\par
                  <li>Equating water baptism with Spirit baptism.</li>\par
                  <li>Teaching that tongues are the mandatory evidence for all.</li>\par
                  <li>Treating Spirit baptism as a "second-tier" upgrade rather than universal to believers.</li>\par
                  <li>Neglecting the necessity of Spirit-filled living.</li>\par
              </ul>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <p><strong>FINAL SUMMARY</strong></p>\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-sm">\par
              <div className="border p-3 rounded">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-blue-700">Water Baptism</p>\par
                  <p>External, symbolic, commanded, and public testimony.</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="border p-3 rounded">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-purple-700">Spirit Baptism</p>\par
                  <p>Internal, regenerative, incorporating, and universal at conversion.</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\{\par
  id: 10,\par
  title: 'LESSON 10: BAPTISM INTO THE BODY OF CHRIST',\par
  description: 'Exploring the spiritual placement of the believer into union with Christ and the resulting unity of the Church.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p><strong>I. SPIRITUAL PLACEMENT INTO CHRIST</strong></p>\par
          <p>The most explicit text on this doctrine is <ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 12:13" />:</p>\par
          <blockquote className="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4 italic py-2">\par
              \ldblquote For in one Spirit we were all baptized into one body \emdash  Jews or Greeks, slaves or free \emdash  and all were made to drink of one Spirit.\rdblquote\par
          </blockquote>\par
          <p>This baptism is not water baptism; it is the Spirit\rquote s act of placing the believer into union with Christ. This involves:</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">\par
              <li><strong>The Spirit</strong> as the agent.</li>\par
              <li><strong>The believer</strong> as the recipient.</li>\par
              <li><strong>The Body of Christ</strong> as the sphere.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <div className="mt-4 space-y-4">\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold">1. What Does \ldblquote Into Christ\rdblquote  Mean?</p>\par
                  <p>To be placed \ldblquote into Christ\rdblquote  is a spiritual relocation from being "in Adam" (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 15:22" />) to being "in Christ" (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 1:3\endash 14" />). This means:</p>\par
                  <ul className="list-disc pl-5 mt-2">\par
                      <li>Union with Him in death and resurrection (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:3\endash 5" />).</li>\par
                      <li>Sharing in His righteousness (<ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 5:21" />).</li>\par
                      <li>Being seated with Him spiritually (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:5\endash 6" />).</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold">2. Union with Christ Is the Foundation of All Blessings</p>\par
                  <p>Every spiritual blessing flows from this union (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 1:3" />). Because we are in Him, we are justified (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:1" />), adopted (<ScriptureText text="Galatians 4:4\endash 7" />), and secure (<ScriptureText text="John 10:28\endash 29" />).</p>\par
              </section>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. UNITY OF BELIEVERS</strong></p>\par
          <p>If all believers are baptized into one body, then unity is not optional\emdash it is a spiritual reality.</p>\par
          \par
          <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-4">\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>One Body, Many Members:</strong> Just as a physical body has different parts, the Church has one Head (Christ) and many members (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 12:12; Colossians 1:18" />).\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Unity Transcends Human Distinctions:</strong> Spirit baptism destroys spiritual hierarchy based on ethnicity, status, or background (<ScriptureText text="Galatians 3:28" />). The church is united by shared union with Christ, not shared culture.\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Unity Is Spiritual Before It Is Visible:</strong> The \ldblquote one baptism\rdblquote  in <ScriptureText text="Ephesians 4:4\endash 5" /> refers primarily to Spirit baptism\emdash the unifying act that joins all believers to Christ.\par
              </li>\par
          </ol>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>III. SPIRITUAL IDENTITY</strong></p>\par
          <p>Baptism into the body of Christ reshapes who we are. The believer's primary identity is now \ldblquote In Christ.\rdblquote </p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3">\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>A New Creation:</strong> This is an ontological change, not mere moral reform (<ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 5:17" />).\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>A Member of the Body:</strong> No believer is spiritually independent; every member is necessary (<ScriptureText text="Romans 12:5; 1 Corinthians 12:21" />).\par
              </li>\par
              <li>\par
                  <strong>Christ\rquote s Possession:</strong> We are not our own; we are bought with a price and represent Him (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 6:19\endash 20" />).\par
              </li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS</strong></p>\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-md">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Destroys Individualism</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">Spirit baptism declares interdependence. Isolation contradicts our spiritual placement.</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-md">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Produces Humility</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">Since placement is by grace, there is no room for boasting or "superior" classes of Christians.</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-md">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Demands Love</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">We are commanded to maintain the unity the Spirit has already created (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 4:3" />).</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-md">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Encourages Assurance</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">Our security rests in the Body we have been joined to, not our own efforts (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:38\endash 39" />).</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="mt-6 border-t pt-4">\par
              <p><strong>FINAL SUMMARY</strong></p>\par
              <div className="overflow-x-auto mt-2">\par
                  <table className="min-w-full text-sm border">\par
                      <thead>\par
                          <tr className="bg-gray-100">\par
                              <th className="border p-2">Aspect</th>\par
                              <th className="border p-2">Water Baptism</th>\par
                              <th className="border p-2">Body (Spirit) Baptism</th>\par
                          </tr>\par
                      </thead>\par
                      <tbody>\par
                          <tr>\par
                              <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Nature</td>\par
                              <td className="border p-2">External & Symbolic</td>\par
                              <td className="border p-2">Internal & Real Placement</td>\par
                          </tr>\par
                          <tr>\par
                              <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Effect</td>\par
                              <td className="border p-2">Public Declaration</td>\par
                              <td className="border p-2">Union with Christ</td>\par
                          </tr>\par
                          <tr>\par
                              <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Scope</td>\par
                              <td className="border p-2">Local Church Identification</td>\par
                              <td className="border p-2">Universal Body Incorporation</td>\par
                          </tr>\par
                      </tbody>\par
                  </table>\par
              </div>\par
              <p className="mt-4 text-center font-semibold">Baptism into the body of Christ is the foundation of Christian unity and identity.</p>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\{\par
  id: 11,\par
  title: 'LESSON 11: SYMBOLISM & SPIRITUAL REALITIES',\par
  description: 'Understanding the gospel pattern of death, burial, and resurrection as the foundation for the Christian life.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p><strong>I. DEATH, BURIAL, AND RESURRECTION \emdash  THE GOSPEL PATTERN</strong></p>\par
          <p>The gospel is a threefold reality: Christ died, was buried, and rose again (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 15:3\endash 4" />). Baptism visibly portrays this pattern (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:3\endash 4" />), reflecting our spiritual participation in Christ\rquote s work.</p>\par
          \par
          \par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. CRUCIFIED WITH CHRIST</strong></p>\par
          <p><ScriptureText text="Galatians 2:20" /> declares: <em>\ldblquote I have been crucified with Christ. It is no longer I who live, but Christ who lives in me.\rdblquote </em></p>\par
          <div className="pl-5 space-y-3">\par
              <p><strong>1. Positional Crucifixion:</strong> At conversion, our "old self" was crucified to break sin's ruling power (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:6" />). This is a judicial reality\emdash the old identity in Adam is judged and finished.</p>\par
              <p><strong>2. Practical Implications:</strong> Because we are crucified, we do not live under sin\rquote s dominion. We reckon ourselves dead to sin (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:11" />) and renounce our former way of life.</p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>III. BURIED WITH HIM</strong></p>\par
          <p>As stated in <ScriptureText text="Colossians 2:12" />, burial confirms death. In biblical symbolism, immersion represents:</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">\par
              <li>Final separation from the past.</li>\par
              <li>The end of the old identity (you do not bury what is alive).</li>\par
              <li>The fact that the "old man" is not reformed, but buried.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. RAISED TO WALK IN NEWNESS OF LIFE</strong></p>\par
          <p>Christianity is resurrection life. <ScriptureText text="Romans 6:4" /> commands us to <em>\ldblquote walk in newness of life.\rdblquote </em></p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">\par
              <li><strong>Spiritual Resurrection:</strong> We have been made alive together with Christ (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:5\endash 6" />), receiving new affections and a new nature.</li>\par
              <li><strong>Visible Conduct:</strong> "Walk" refers to our daily conduct, manifesting in holiness, righteousness, and love.</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-gray-400">\par
              <p><strong>V. DIE DAILY & VI. CARRY YOUR CROSS</strong></p>\par
              <p className="text-sm mt-2">\par
                  While our crucifixion is positionally complete, there is an experiential daily surrender. \par
                  Paul said, \ldblquote I die every day\rdblquote  (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 15:31" />). \par
                  Jesus taught that taking up the cross daily (<ScriptureText text="Luke 9:23" />) means death to self-will, pride, and worldly ambition. \par
                  <strong>The cross is not decorative; it is execution.</strong>\par
              </p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>VII. MORTIFICATION AND SANCTIFICATION</strong></p>\par
          <p>Identity precedes conduct. Because we have been raised, we must practically "put to death" what is earthly in us (<ScriptureText text="Colossians 3:1\endash 5; Romans 8:13" />). Sanctification flows from our union with Christ.</p>\par
\par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <p><strong>VIII. BAPTISM AS COVENANT DECLARATION</strong></p>\par
              <p>Entering the water is a public declaration: <em>My old life is over, my allegiance has changed, and my future belongs to Christ.</em> It aligns inward reality with outward testimony (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 10:22" />).</p>\par
\par
              <p><strong>IX. SUFFERING AND PERSEVERANCE</strong></p>\par
              <p>Union with Christ includes the "fellowship of his sufferings" (<ScriptureText text="Philippians 3:10" />). Resurrection life doesn't eliminate pain; it empowers us to persevere for His glory (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:17" />).</p>\par
\par
              <p><strong>X. GLORY AHEAD</strong></p>\par
              <p>Baptism looks forward. When Christ appears, we will appear with Him in glory (<ScriptureText text="Colossians 3:4" />). The water represents a transition from death to eternal life.</p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="mt-6 p-4 bg-blue-50 rounded-xl border border-blue-100">\par
              <p className="font-bold text-blue-800 mb-2">FINAL SUMMARY</p>\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-sm">\par
                  <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                      <li><strong>Death:</strong> Positional end of the old self.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Burial:</strong> Decisive break from the past.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Resurrection:</strong> Power for new conduct.</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
                  <ul className="list-disc pl-5">\par
                      <li><strong>Daily Dying:</strong> Continual surrender.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Cross-bearing:</strong> Path of obedience.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Sanctification:</strong> Growing in holiness.</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </div>\par
              <p className="mt-3 text-xs italic text-center">Baptism is a declaration of lifelong crucified living and resurrection power.</p>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\{\par
  id: 12,\par
  title: 'LESSON 12: COVENANT IDENTITY',\par
  description: 'Understanding our new standing in the New Covenant and the authority, freedom, and identity we have in Christ.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p><strong>I. WHAT IS COVENANT IDENTITY?</strong></p>\par
          <p>A covenant is a divinely initiated binding relationship established by God\rquote s promise and sealed by His authority. Under the New Covenant (<ScriptureText text="Luke 22:20; Hebrews 8:6" />), we are defined by our union with Christ rather than our past.</p>\par
          \par
          <p><ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 5:17" /> declares: <em>\ldblquote If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation.\rdblquote </em> This is an ontological transformation\emdash a new spiritual state.</p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. NEW IDENTITY IN CHRIST</strong></p>\par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold underline">1. From Adam to Christ (Covenant Transfer)</p>\par
                  <p>Before salvation, we were "in Adam"\emdash condemned and enslaved to sin. After salvation, we are "in Christ"\emdash justified and reconciled (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 15:22; Romans 5:12" />).</p>\par
                  \par
                  \par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold underline">2. Adopted as Sons and Daughters</p>\par
                  <p>We have received the Spirit of adoption (<ScriptureText text="Galatians 4:6\endash 7" />). This means full acceptance, legal standing, and inheritance rights (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:17" />). We are not spiritual orphans.</p>\par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold underline">3. Righteousness and Royalty</p>\par
                  <p>Righteousness is our <strong>status</strong>, not just our behavior (<ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 5:21" />). We are also called a "royal priesthood" (<ScriptureText text="1 Peter 2:9" />), representing God on earth with dignity and a holy calling.</p>\par
              </section>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>III. AUTHORITY OF THE BELIEVER</strong></p>\par
          <p>Authority flows from our union with Christ. Because He is exalted, we share in His spiritual victory (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 1:20\endash 22; 2:6" />).</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">\par
              <li><strong>Over Sin:</strong> Sin no longer has dominion (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:14" />).</li>\par
              <li><strong>Over the Enemy:</strong> We have authority to resist the devil (<ScriptureText text="James 4:7; Luke 10:19" />).</li>\par
              <li><strong>In Prayer:</strong> We intercede boldly in Christ's name (<ScriptureText text="John 14:13\endash 14" />).</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. FREEDOM FROM BONDAGE</strong></p>\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\par
              <div className="p-3 border rounded-lg bg-green-50">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Freedom from Condemnation</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">Guilt no longer defines those who are in Christ Jesus (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:1" />).</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-3 border rounded-lg bg-green-50">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Freedom from Sin's Mastery</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">The one who has died is freed from habitual sin (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:6\endash 7" />).</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-3 border rounded-lg bg-green-50">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Freedom from the Law's Curse</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">We are redeemed from the curse and live under grace (<ScriptureText text="Galatians 3:13" />).</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-3 border rounded-lg bg-green-50">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Freedom from Fear</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">We have not been given a spirit of fear, but of power and love (<ScriptureText text="2 Timothy 1:7" />).</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>V. LIVING FROM IDENTITY, NOT FOR IDENTITY</strong></p>\par
          <blockquote className="bg-gray-100 p-4 border-l-4 border-gray-500 italic">\par
              "We do not obey to become accepted. We obey because we are accepted."\par
          </blockquote>\par
          <p>Identity precedes conduct. We must renew our minds to align our daily walk with the covenant truth (<ScriptureText text="Romans 12:2" />).</p>\par
\par
          <p><strong>VI. DANGERS OF IDENTITY AMNESIA</strong></p>\par
          <p>Believers who live in shame or defeat often suffer from "spiritual amnesia." Understanding covenant identity stabilizes the believer against the feeling of spiritual inferiority (<ScriptureText text="Hosea 4:6" />).</p>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-xl mt-6 border border-blue-200">\par
              <p><strong>FINAL SUMMARY</strong></p>\par
              <p className="text-sm mt-2">Covenant identity provides:</p>\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 mt-2 text-xs font-semibold">\par
                  <div className="bg-white p-2 rounded shadow-sm">NEW CREATION</div>\par
                  <div className="bg-white p-2 rounded shadow-sm">ADOPTED CHILD</div>\par
                  <div className="bg-white p-2 rounded shadow-sm">RIGHTEOUS STATUS</div>\par
                  <div className="bg-white p-2 rounded shadow-sm">SEATED WITH CHRIST</div>\par
                  <div className="bg-white p-2 rounded shadow-sm">NO CONDEMNATION</div>\par
                  <div className="bg-white p-2 rounded shadow-sm">VICTORY OVER SIN</div>\par
              </div>\par
              <p className="mt-4 text-sm italic">Baptism declares this identity publicly; daily living must align with this reality.</p>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\{\par
  id: 13,\par
  title: 'LESSON 13: SPIRITUAL WARFARE AND NEW LIFE',\par
  description: 'Preparing the believer for the spiritual opposition that often follows the public declaration of baptism.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p className="italic text-gray-600">\par
              Many new believers assume that after baptism, life becomes easier. In reality, baptism often marks the beginning of intensified spiritual opposition.\par
          </p>\par
          \par
          <p>Jesus was baptized (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 3:16\endash 17" />), and immediately He was led into the wilderness to be tempted (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 4:1" />). That sequence is instructive for us today.</p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>I. UNDERSTANDING ATTACKS AFTER BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
          <p>Baptism publicly declares allegiance to Christ, which draws attention in the spiritual realm. <ScriptureText text="1 Peter 5:8" /> warns that our adversary, the devil, prowls like a roaring lion.</p>\par
          \par
          <div className="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-red-400">\par
              <p className="font-bold text-red-800 mb-2">Types of Attacks New Believers Face:</p>\par
              <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm">\par
                  <li><strong>Doubt:</strong> "Are you really saved?" The enemy attacks identity just as he did with Jesus (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 4:3" />).</li>\par
                  <li><strong>Intensified Temptation:</strong> Old habits may resurface. Temptation does not mean an absence of salvation, but a call to reliance (<ScriptureText text="James 1:14" />).</li>\par
                  <li><strong>Social Pressure:</strong> Resistance from friends, family, or culture (<ScriptureText text="1 Peter 4:4" />).</li>\par
                  <li><strong>Accusation:</strong> The enemy is the "accuser," but <ScriptureText text="Romans 8:33\endash 34" /> reminds us that no charge can stand against God\rquote s elect.</li>\par
              </ul>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. THE TEMPTATION TO GO BACK</strong></p>\par
          <p>The greatest danger is retreat. Like Israel longing for Egypt (<ScriptureText text="Numbers 14:3\endash 4" />), new believers may be tempted by comfort or familiarity. However, Jesus warned that those who look back are not fit for the kingdom (<ScriptureText text="Luke 9:62" />). We are those who have faith, not those who shrink back (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 10:39" />).</p>\par
\par
          <p><strong>III. STANDING FIRM IN FAITH</strong></p>\par
          <p>Spiritual warfare is fought in God's strength, not ours. We are commanded to "put on the whole armor of God" (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 6:11" />):</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-1 text-sm">\par
              <li>Belt of Truth</li>\par
              <li>Breastplate of Righteousness</li>\par
              <li>Gospel of Peace</li>\par
              <li>Shield of Faith</li>\par
              <li>Helmet of Salvation</li>\par
              <li>Sword of the Spirit (The Word)</li>\par
          </ul>\par
          <p className="mt-2 text-sm">Victory begins with <strong>submission to God</strong>, which allows us to effectively resist the devil (<ScriptureText text="James 4:7" />).</p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <section>\par
                  <p><strong>IV. THE ROLE OF PRAYER & V. THE WORD OF GOD</strong></p>\par
                  <p>Prayer sustains spiritual strength and invites divine help (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 4:16" />). Coupled with the Word\emdash our only offensive weapon\emdash we can respond to lies with "It is written," just as Jesus did (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 4:4" />). The mind must be renewed (<ScriptureText text="Romans 12:2" />) to replace old patterns with truth.</p>\par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section>\par
                  <p><strong>VI. IMPORTANCE OF FELLOWSHIP</strong></p>\par
                  <p>Isolation weakens believers. We need teaching, encouragement, and accountability. A cord of three strands is not easily broken (<ScriptureText text="Ecclesiastes 4:12" />), and the local body provides protection (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 10:24\endash 25" />).</p>\par
              </section>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IX. PRACTICAL INSTRUCTIONS FOR NEW BELIEVERS</strong></p>\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3">\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-white border rounded shadow-sm">Establish daily prayer and Bible reading.</div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-white border rounded shadow-sm">Join consistent fellowship immediately.</div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-white border rounded shadow-sm">Remove environments that fuel temptation.</div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-white border rounded shadow-sm">Confess sin quickly (<ScriptureText text="1 John 1:9" />).</div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-white border rounded shadow-sm">Guard the mind (<ScriptureText text="Philippians 4:8" />).</div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-white border rounded shadow-sm">Remember your identity in Christ (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:1" />).</div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="mt-6 p-4 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-lg">\par
              <p className="font-bold text-center mb-2">FINAL SUMMARY</p>\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs opacity-90">\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="font-bold text-red-400">EXPECT OPPOSITION:</p>\par
                      <p>Doubt, temptation, testing, and social pressure.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="font-bold text-green-400">EXPECT VICTORY:</p>\par
                      <p>Spirit's help, power of the Word, and the support of the Church.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </div>\par
              <p className="mt-4 text-center text-sm border-t border-gray-700 pt-2 italic">\par
                  "He who is in you is greater than he who is in the world." \emdash  <ScriptureText text="1 John 4:4" />\par
              </p>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\par
\{\par
  id: 14,\par
  title: 'LESSON 14: THE CHRISTIAN LIFE AFTER BAPTISM',\par
  description: 'A guide to intentional spiritual growth, the essential disciplines, and maturing into Christ\rquote s likeness.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p>\par
              Baptism marks identification with Christ, but the life that follows is about transformation. \par
              <ScriptureText text="Romans 8:29" /> teaches that believers are predestined <em>\ldblquote to be conformed to the image of His Son.\rdblquote </em> \par
              Spiritual growth is not automatic\emdash it is intentional, Spirit-enabled development.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>I. GROWING SPIRITUALLY (SANCTIFICATION)</strong></p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">\par
              <li><strong>Growth Is Expected:</strong> Just as physical life requires nourishment, spiritual life requires cultivation (<ScriptureText text="2 Peter 3:18; Hebrews 5:12\endash 14" />).</li>\par
              <li><strong>Growth Is Progressive:</strong> Maturity happens gradually. <ScriptureText text="Philippians 1:6" /> assures us that He who began the work will complete it.</li>\par
              <li><strong>Growth Requires Cooperation:</strong> While God supplies grace, we must respond through spiritual disciplines (<ScriptureText text="Philippians 2:12\endash 13" />).</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">\par
              <section className="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-100">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2">II. PRAYER LIFE</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm mb-2">Prayer is communion with God\emdash a relationship, not just a list of requests (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 6:9\endash 13" />).</p>\par
                  <ul className="list-disc pl-5 text-xs space-y-1">\par
                      <li><strong>Adoration:</strong> Praising God's character.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Confession:</strong> Agreeing with God about sin.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Thanksgiving:</strong> Gratitude for His work.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Supplication:</strong> Bringing requests to Him.</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section className="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-green-100">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-green-900 mb-2">III. BIBLE STUDY</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm mb-2">The Word of God is spiritual nourishment that equips us for every good work (<ScriptureText text="2 Timothy 3:16\endash 17" />).</p>\par
                  <ul className="list-disc pl-5 text-xs space-y-1">\par
                      <li><strong>Read:</strong> Consistently and broadly.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Observe:</strong> What does the text say?</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Interpret:</strong> What does it mean in context?</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Apply:</strong> How should I respond personally?</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </section>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. FELLOWSHIP & V. WORSHIP</strong></p>\par
          <div className="space-y-3">\par
              <p>\par
                  <strong>Fellowship:</strong> Christianity is not solitary. We are placed into a Body (<ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 12:13" />) for encouragement, accountability, and service. Growth accelerates when we serve others within the church (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 10:24\endash 25" />).\par
              </p>\par
              <p>\par
                  <strong>Worship:</strong> More than music, worship is a lifestyle. <ScriptureText text="Romans 12:1" /> describes presenting our bodies as a <em>\ldblquote living sacrifice.\rdblquote </em> True worshipers worship in spirit and truth\emdash with sincere hearts aligned with God's Word (<ScriptureText text="John 4:23\endash 24" />).\par
              </p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="mt-6 border-2 border-gray-200 p-4 rounded-xl">\par
              <p className="font-bold text-center mb-4">VI. BALANCED CHRISTIAN DEVELOPMENT</p>\par
              <div className="flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-around items-center space-y-4 sm:space-y-0 text-center text-sm">\par
                  <div>\par
                      <div className="w-16 h-16 bg-blue-600 text-white rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mx-auto mb-2">\f3\u8593?\f0 </div>\par
                      <p className="font-bold">UPWARD</p>\par
                      <p className="text-xs">Worship</p>\par
                  </div>\par
                  <div className="hidden sm:block text-gray-400">\f3\u8594?\f0 </div>\par
                  <div>\par
                      <div className="w-16 h-16 bg-green-600 text-white rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mx-auto mb-2">\f5\lang1033\u8596?\f0 </div>\par
                      <p className="font-bold">INWARD</p>\par
                      <p className="text-xs">Word & Prayer</p>\par
                  </div>\par
                  <div className="hidden sm:block text-gray-400">\f3\u8594?\f0 </div>\par
                  <div>\par
                      <div className="w-16 h-16 bg-purple-600 text-white rounded-full flex items-center justify-center mx-auto mb-2">\f3\u8595?\f0 </div>\par
                      <p className="font-bold">OUTWARD</p>\par
                      <p className="text-xs">Fellowship & Service</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>VII. COMMON CHALLENGES AFTER BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
          <p className="text-sm">\par
              Expect challenges like <strong>inconsistency, spiritual dryness, and distraction.</strong> The solution is not to quit, but to persevere and return to the foundational disciplines.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-gray-800 text-white p-5 rounded-lg mt-6 shadow-xl">\par
              <p className="text-center font-bold text-lg mb-3 tracking-wide">FINAL SUMMARY</p>\par
              <p className="text-center text-sm opacity-90 mb-4">After baptism, your life is defined by:</p>\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs font-mono">\par
                  <div className="border border-gray-600 p-2 text-center uppercase">Intentional Growth</div>\par
                  <div className="border border-gray-600 p-2 text-center uppercase">Regular Prayer</div>\par
                  <div className="border border-gray-600 p-2 text-center uppercase">Consistent Study</div>\par
                  <div className="border border-gray-600 p-2 text-center uppercase">Active Fellowship</div>\par
              </div>\par
              <p className="mt-5 text-center text-sm italic border-t border-gray-600 pt-3">\par
                  Baptism declares new life; spiritual disciplines develop that life.\par
              </p>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\{\par
  id: 15,\par
  title: 'LESSON 15: THE ROLE OF THE CHURCH & RESPONSIBILITIES OF NEW BELIEVERS',\par
  description: 'Understanding the local church as Christ\f1\rquote\f0 s body, the necessity of spiritual covering, and the responsibilities of a committed believer.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p>\par
              Baptism brings a person into Christ\f1\emdash but it also brings them into His Body. \par
              Christianity is personal, but it is never private.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>I. THE IMPORTANCE OF THE LOCAL CHURCH</strong></p>\par
          <div className="space-y-3">\par
              <p>\par
                  <strong>The Church Is Christ\rquote s Body:</strong> In <ScriptureText text="1 Corinthians 12:27" />, Paul says: <em>\ldblquote Now you are the body of Christ and individually members of it.\rdblquote </em> The Church is not a building; it is a spiritual organism.\par
              </p>\par
              <p>\par
                  <strong>Christ\rquote s Project:</strong> Jesus declared, <em>\ldblquote I will build My church\rdblquote </em> (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 16:18" />). To honor Christ is to honor what He is building.\par
              </p>\par
              <p>\par
                  <strong>The Early Church Pattern:</strong> <ScriptureText text="Acts 2:42" /> shows believers devoted to apostolic teaching, fellowship, breaking of bread, and prayer. They did not drift independently; they committed.\par
              </p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. ACCOUNTABILITY & III. SPIRITUAL COVERING</strong></p>\par
          <div className="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-blue-500">\par
              <p className="font-bold text-blue-900 mb-2">Why Oversight Matters:</p>\par
              <p className="text-sm mb-2">\par
                  Accountability protects us from pride and hidden sin (<ScriptureText text="Proverbs 27:17" />). \par
                  Spiritual leaders are shepherds who keep watch over your soul (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 13:17" />).\par
              </p>\par
              <ul className="list-disc pl-5 text-xs space-y-1">\par
                  <li><strong>Protection:</strong> Guidance prevents unnecessary spiritual damage.</li>\par
                  <li><strong>Instruction:</strong> Correcting doctrinal and moral blind spots.</li>\par
                  <li><strong>Order:</strong> God works through divinely appointed structure, not chaos.</li>\par
              </ul>\par
              <p className="mt-2 text-xs italic">Note: Biblical covering protects freedom; it does not manipulate or dominate.</p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. RESPONSIBILITIES OF NEW BELIEVERS</strong></p>\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\par
              <section className="p-3 border rounded shadow-sm">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">1. Commitment</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">Stop "church hopping." Those planted in the house of the Lord shall flourish (<ScriptureText text="Psalm 92:13" />).</p>\par
              </section>\par
              <section className="p-3 border rounded shadow-sm">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">2. Faithfulness</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">This includes regular attendance, financial stewardship (giving), and respect for leadership.</p>\par
              </section>\par
              <section className="p-3 border rounded shadow-sm">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">3. Holiness</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">Your conduct reflects the testimony of Christ to the world (<ScriptureText text="1 Peter 2:12" />).</p>\par
              </section>\par
              <section className="p-3 border rounded shadow-sm">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">4. Service</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">Every believer has spiritual gifts intended for the edification of the whole body (<ScriptureText text="Romans 12:4\endash 8" />).</p>\par
              </section>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>V. COMMON ATTITUDES TO AVOID</strong></p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm">\par
              <li><strong>Consumer Mentality:</strong> Attending only for personal benefit. The church is not a supermarket.</li>\par
              <li><strong>Independent Spirit:</strong> Thinking "It's just me and God." This contradicts the biblical design.</li>\par
              <li><strong>Offense & Leaving:</strong> Maturity requires forgiveness and humility rather than running from correction.</li>\par
              <li><strong>Neglect of Giving:</strong> Generosity is an act of worship and stewardship (<ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 9:7" />).</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <p><strong>VI. UNITY IN THE BODY</strong></p>\par
          <p>\par
              Unity requires humility, patience, and bearing with one another in love (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 4:3" />). \par
              Responsible believers protect the peace of the family.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-gray-900 text-white p-6 rounded-xl mt-8">\par
              <p className="text-center font-bold text-xl mb-4">FINAL SUMMARY</p>\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-sm">\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="text-blue-400 font-bold underline mb-2">THE CHURCH IS:</p>\par
                      <ul className="space-y-1 opacity-90">\par
                          <li>\bullet  Christ\rquote s Body</li>\par
                          <li>\bullet  God\rquote s Design</li>\par
                          <li>\bullet  A place of protection</li>\par
                      </ul>\par
                  </div>\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="text-green-400 font-bold underline mb-2">BELIEVERS MUST:</p>\par
                      <ul className="space-y-1 opacity-90">\par
                          <li>\bullet  Commit & Serve</li>\par
                          <li>\bullet  Submit to Guidance</li>\par
                          <li>\bullet  Protect Unity</li>\par
                      </ul>\par
                  </div>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="mt-6 pt-4 border-t border-gray-700 text-center">\par
                  <p className="italic text-sm">\ldblquote Baptism begins the journey. Church commitment sustains it.\rdblquote </p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\par
\{\par
  id: 16,\par
  title: 'LESSON 16: CHRISTIAN CHARACTER',\par
  description: 'Developing Christlikeness through the fruit of the Spirit, integrity, holiness, and a heart of forgiveness.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p>\par
              Baptism identifies a believer with Christ, but <strong>character</strong> reveals whether Christ is being formed within. \par
              God is not merely producing activity\emdash He is conforming us to the image of His Son (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:29" />).\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>I. FRUIT OF THE SPIRIT</strong></p>\par
          <p>Christian character is not self-manufactured morality; it is the product of the Holy Spirit. In <ScriptureText text="Galatians 5:22\endash 23" />, Paul lists the Fruit of the Spirit:</p>\par
          \par
          \par
\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mt-4 text-sm">\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-green-50 rounded-lg">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Fruit vs. Gifts</p>\par
                  <p>Spiritual gifts demonstrate <strong>ability</strong>; spiritual fruit demonstrates <strong>maturity</strong>. Character sustains what charisma cannot.</p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-green-50 rounded-lg">\par
                  <p className="font-bold">Cultivation</p>\par
                  <p>Fruit grows through abiding in Christ (<ScriptureText text="John 15:4\endash 5" />), yielding to the Spirit, and enduring trials.</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. INTEGRITY & III. HOLINESS</strong></p>\par
          <div className="space-y-3">\par
              <p>\par
                  <strong>Integrity</strong> means wholeness\emdash being the same in private and public. God sees what others do not (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 4:13" />). Character sustains your witness in financial dealings, relationships, and online behavior.\par
              </p>\par
              <p>\par
                  <strong>Holiness</strong> means being set apart for God (<ScriptureText text="1 Peter 1:15\endash 16" />). It is not legalism or religious performance; it is an inward transformation that affects our desires, entertainment, and speech.\par
              </p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. FORGIVENESS</strong></p>\par
          <p>Believers forgive because they have been forgiven (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 6:14\endash 15" />). Unforgiveness breeds bitterness and blocks spiritual growth (<ScriptureText text="Hebrews 12:15" />).</p>\par
          <div className="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-yellow-400 text-sm">\par
              <p className="font-bold mb-1 text-yellow-900">What Forgiveness Is and Is Not:</p>\par
              <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">\par
                  <li><strong>It IS:</strong> Releasing the offender to God and refusing revenge.</li>\par
                  <li><strong>It IS NOT:</strong> Ignoring consequences or instantly restoring trust (which must be earned).</li>\par
              </ul>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>VI. MARKS OF A MATURE CHRISTIAN</strong></p>\par
          <p className="text-sm italic">Maturity is not measured by years in church, but by transformation. A maturing believer:</p>\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 text-sm">\par
              <div className="flex items-center space-x-2">\par
                  <span className="text-green-600">\f5\lang1033\u10003?\f0 </span>\par
                  <span>Responds rather than reacts</span>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="flex items-center space-x-2">\par
                  <span className="text-green-600">\f5\u10003?\f0 </span>\par
                  <span>Seeks peace</span>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="flex items-center space-x-2">\par
                  <span className="text-green-600">\f5\u10003?\f0 </span>\par
                  <span>Admits wrong quickly</span>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="flex items-center space-x-2">\par
                  <span className="text-green-600">\f5\u10003?\f0 </span>\par
                  <span>Receives correction humbly</span>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-gray-900 text-white p-6 rounded-xl mt-8">\par
              <p className="text-center font-bold text-xl mb-4">FINAL SUMMARY</p>\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-sm">\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="text-blue-400 font-bold underline mb-2">CHARACTER IS:</p>\par
                      <ul className="space-y-1 opacity-90">\par
                          <li>\f1\bullet  The Fruit of the Spirit</li>\par
                          <li>\bullet  Integrity in all things</li>\par
                          <li>\bullet  The pursuit of holiness</li>\par
                      </ul>\par
                  </div>\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="text-purple-400 font-bold underline mb-2">PRACTICAL STEPS:</p>\par
                      <ul className="space-y-1 opacity-90">\par
                          <li>\bullet  Walk with the Spirit daily</li>\par
                          <li>\bullet  Confess sin quickly (<ScriptureText text="1 John 1:9" />)</li>\par
                          <li>\bullet  Practice forgiveness</li>\par
                      </ul>\par
                  </div>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="mt-6 pt-4 border-t border-gray-700 text-center">\par
                  <p className="italic text-sm">\ldblquote Gifts may open doors, but character keeps them open.\rdblquote </p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\par
\{\par
  id: 17,\par
  title: 'LESSON 17: STEWARDSHIP & DISCIPLESHIP',\par
  description: 'Managing what God has entrusted to us through our time, talents, and resources, and fulfilling the mission to make disciples.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p>\par
              Salvation is a gift, but everything that follows requires stewardship. \par
              A steward is someone entrusted with something that belongs to another (<ScriptureText text="Psalm 24:1" />). \par
              We own nothing\emdash we manage what belongs to God.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>I. STEWARDSHIP: TIME, TALENT, AND TREASURE</strong></p>\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">\par
              <div className="p-4 bg-blue-50 border rounded-lg">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-blue-900 border-b border-blue-200 mb-2">TIME</p>\par
                  <p className="text-xs">\par
                      Redeem the time (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 5:15\endash 16" />). \par
                      Stewardship means prioritizing God first and avoiding wasteful living. \par
                      <strong>Does your schedule reflect your faith?</strong>\par
                  </p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-4 bg-green-50 border rounded-lg">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-green-900 border-b border-green-200 mb-2">TALENT</p>\par
                  <p className="text-xs">\par
                      Based on the Parable of the Talents (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 25:14\endash 30" />), \par
                      neglect is unacceptable. God expects us to multiply our natural skills and spiritual gifts for His glory.\par
                  </p>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-4 bg-yellow-50 border rounded-lg">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-yellow-900 border-b border-yellow-200 mb-2">TREASURE</p>\par
                  <p className="text-xs">\par
                      Money reveals priorities (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 6:21" />). \par
                      Stewardship includes generosity and supporting ministry with a cheerful heart (<ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 9:7" />).\par
                  </p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <section>\par
                  <p><strong>II. EVANGELISM: THE OUTWARD FACE</strong></p>\par
                  <p>\par
                      The Great Commission (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:19\endash 20" />) is not optional. \par
                      Evangelism is proclaiming the Good News and sharing your testimony. \par
                      Your life should provoke questions that lead to an answer for your hope (<ScriptureText text="1 Peter 3:15" />).\par
                  </p>\par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section>\par
                  <p><strong>III. MAKING DISCIPLES</strong></p>\par
                  <p>Conversion is a moment; discipleship is a process. A disciple is a learner who denies self and follows Christ daily (<ScriptureText text="Luke 9:23" />).</p>\par
                  <ul className="list-disc pl-5 text-sm space-y-1">\par
                      <li><strong>Mentoring:</strong> Modeling obedience to others.</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Teaching:</strong> Passing on scriptural truth (<ScriptureText text="2 Timothy 2:2" />).</li>\par
                      <li><strong>Accountability:</strong> Encouraging growth in others.</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </section>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. LIVING AS AMBASSADORS OF CHRIST</strong></p>\par
          <blockquote className="border-l-4 border-gray-400 pl-4 italic bg-gray-50 py-2">\par
              \ldblquote Therefore, we are ambassadors for Christ, God making his appeal through us...\rdblquote  \emdash  <ScriptureText text="2 Corinthians 5:20" />\par
          </blockquote>\par
          <p className="text-sm">\par
              An ambassador represents a kingdom in a foreign territory. Practically, this means:\par
          </p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 text-sm grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-1">\par
              <li>Reflecting Christ\rquote s character</li>\par
              <li>Guarding our speech</li>\par
              <li>Pursuing reconciliation</li>\par
              <li>Aligning with Scripture over culture</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <div className="mt-6 p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-lg">\par
              <p className="font-bold text-red-800 text-sm">Common Failures to Avoid:</p>\par
              <p className="text-xs text-red-700">Spiritual passivity, fear of rejection, and busyness without mission. Remember: each will give an account to God (<ScriptureText text="Romans 14:12" />).</p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-gray-900 text-white p-6 rounded-xl mt-8">\par
              <p className="text-center font-bold text-xl mb-4 tracking-widest">FINAL SUMMARY</p>\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-sm">\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="text-blue-400 font-bold underline mb-2">STEWARDSHIP:</p>\par
                      <p className="opacity-90">Time wisely used, talents faithfully exercised, and treasure generously given.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="text-green-400 font-bold underline mb-2">DISCIPLESHIP:</p>\par
                      <p className="opacity-90">Sharing the gospel, teaching others, and modeling the Christ-life.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="mt-6 pt-4 border-t border-gray-700 text-center">\par
                  <p className="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Ambassadors for the King</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\par
\{\par
  id: 18,\par
  title: 'LESSON 18: PRACTICAL PREPARATION FOR BAPTISM',\par
  description: 'The spiritual prerequisites and practical logistics to ensure your baptismal service is authentic, reverent, and smooth.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p>\par
              Baptism is a public declaration of inward transformation. Proper preparation ensures that the act is authentic, reverent, and spiritually significant.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>I. REQUIREMENTS FOR BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold underline">1. Personal Confession & Understanding</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">\par
                      Baptism is for those who have personally accepted Jesus as Lord (<ScriptureText text="Romans 10:9\endash 10" />). \par
                      You should be able to articulate the Gospel: sin, God's grace, Christ's sacrifice, and salvation by faith (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:8\endash 9" />).\par
                  </p>\par
              </section>\par
\par
              <section>\par
                  <p className="font-bold underline">2. Repentance & Commitment</p>\par
                  <p className="text-sm">\par
                      Repentance is a decisive turn from sin to God (<ScriptureText text="Acts 3:19" />). \par
                      Baptism is a commitment to "take up your cross daily" (<ScriptureText text="Luke 9:23" />). \par
                      Examine your heart and renounce ongoing sin before the service.\par
                  </p>\par
              </section>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. BAPTISMAL SERVICE PROCEDURE</strong></p>\par
          <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-gray-50 border rounded">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-sm mb-1">What to Wear:</p>\par
                  <ul className="text-xs list-disc pl-4 space-y-1">\par
                      <li>Modest, simple clothing (often a white robe/garment).</li>\par
                      <li>Avoid jewelry or heavy makeup.</li>\par
                      <li>Bring a towel and a full change of dry clothes.</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="p-3 bg-gray-50 border rounded">\par
                  <p className="font-bold text-sm mb-1">Your Testimony:</p>\par
                  <ul className="text-xs list-disc pl-4 space-y-1">\par
                      <li>Keep it brief (1\endash 2 minutes).</li>\par
                      <li>Focus on: Life before Christ, how you met Him, and your commitment to follow Him.</li>\par
                  </ul>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="mt-4">\par
              <p className="font-bold">The Order of Service:</p>\par
              <p className="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-2">Most services follow this spiritual flow:</p>\par
              <ol className="list-decimal pl-5 text-sm space-y-1">\par
                  <li>Welcome & Opening Prayer</li>\par
                  <li>Scripture Reading (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:19\endash 20" />)</li>\par
                  <li>Public Confession of Faith</li>\par
                  <li>Immersion in Water</li>\par
                  <li>Congregational Celebration & Blessing</li>\par
                  <li>Post-baptism Discipleship Instructions</li>\par
              </ol>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>III. SUMMARY CHECKLIST FOR NEW BELIEVERS</strong></p>\par
          <div className="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-200">\par
              <div className="space-y-3">\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="font-bold text-blue-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Before Baptism:</p>\par
                      <ul className="space-y-1 text-sm">\par
                          <li className="flex items-start">\par
                              <input type="checkbox" className="mt-1 mr-2" /> <span>Personal salvation confirmed & Gospel understood.</span>\par
                          </li>\par
                          <li className="flex items-start">\par
                              <input type="checkbox" className="mt-1 mr-2" /> <span>Heart truly repentant and committed to Christ.</span>\par
                          </li>\par
                          <li className="flex items-start">\par
                              <input type="checkbox" className="mt-1 mr-2" /> <span>Practical items ready (Clothes, towel, footwear).</span>\par
                          </li>\par
                      </ul>\par
                  </div>\par
                  <div>\par
                      <p className="font-bold text-blue-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">During Baptism:</p>\par
                      <ul className="space-y-1 text-sm">\par
                          <li className="flex items-start">\par
                              <input type="checkbox" className="mt-1 mr-2" /> <span>Arrive early and follow pastor's instructions.</span>\par
                          </li>\par
                          <li className="flex items-start">\par
                              <input type="checkbox" className="mt-1 mr-2" /> <span>Participate reverently and confess faith audibly.</span>\par
                          </li>\par
                      </ul>\par
                  </div>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-gray-900 text-white p-6 rounded-xl mt-8">\par
              <p className="text-center font-bold text-xl mb-2">FINAL WORDS</p>\par
              <p className="text-center text-sm opacity-90">\par
                  Baptism is the gateway from learning to living, from private faith to public obedience. \par
                  A well-prepared believer experiences peace, joy, and confidence in this holy act.\par
              </p>\par
              <div className="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-700 text-center">\par
                  <p className="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-blue-400">Welcome to the Family of God</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\{\par
  id: 19,\par
  title: 'LESSON 19: WRITING PERSONAL TESTIMONY',\par
  description: 'Developing a clear, concise story of your journey to Christ and grounding it in the biblical assurance of salvation.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p>\par
              A personal testimony is a story of God\rquote s work in your life. It demonstrates assurance of salvation, shows transformation, and equips you to share your faith confidently with others.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>I. ASSURANCE OF SALVATION</strong></p>\par
          <p className="text-sm">Assurance is confidence in your relationship with Christ based on God's Word, not fluctuating feelings.</p>\par
          <ul className="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm">\par
              <li><strong>The Promise:</strong> Believers have eternal life the moment they believe (<ScriptureText text="John 5:24" />).</li>\par
              <li><strong>The Purpose:</strong> Scripture was written so that you may <em>know</em> you have eternal life (<ScriptureText text="1 John 5:13" />).</li>\par
              <li><strong>The Witness:</strong> The Holy Spirit bears witness with our spirit that we are children of God (<ScriptureText text="Romans 8:16" />).</li>\par
          </ul>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. COMPONENTS OF A PERSONAL TESTIMONY</strong></p>\par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <div className="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">\par
                  <section className="p-3 bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-blue-400">\par
                      <p className="font-bold text-sm">1. Life Before Christ</p>\par
                      <p className="text-xs text-gray-600 italic">"Before I met Christ, I felt lost and sought fulfillment in things that never satisfied."</p>\par
                  </section>\par
                  <section className="p-3 bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-green-400">\par
                      <p className="font-bold text-sm">2. How You Met Christ</p>\par
                      <p className="text-xs text-gray-600 italic">"I realized I could not save myself. I prayed and invited Jesus to take control of my life."</p>\par
                  </section>\par
                  <section className="p-3 bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-purple-400">\par
                      <p className="font-bold text-sm">3. Changes After Salvation</p>\par
                      <p className="text-xs text-gray-600 italic">"Since trusting Christ, I am more patient, I forgive readily, and I have a desire to serve."</p>\par
                  </section>\par
                  <section className="p-3 bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-yellow-400">\par
                      <p className="font-bold text-sm">4. Commitment Forward</p>\par
                      <p className="text-xs text-gray-600 italic">"I commit to growing in Christ daily and sharing His love with others."</p>\par
                  </section>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>III. PRACTICAL GUIDE TO WRITING</strong></p>\par
          <div className="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-100">\par
              <ul className="list-disc pl-5 text-sm space-y-1">\par
                  <li><strong>Be Concise:</strong> Aim for 1\endash 3 minutes when spoken (about one page written).</li>\par
                  <li><strong>Be Truthful:</strong> Honesty resonates more than exaggeration.</li>\par
                  <li><strong>Use "I" Statements:</strong> Make it personal and authentic.</li>\par
                  <li><strong>Focus on Transformation:</strong> Highlight God\rquote s work, not just your effort.</li>\par
              </ul>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. SAMPLE OUTLINE</strong></p>\par
          <div className="border p-4 rounded-lg text-sm bg-white shadow-sm space-y-2">\par
              <p><strong>Assurance:</strong> "I know I am saved because I have confessed Jesus as Lord and believed in His resurrection."</p>\par
              <p><strong>Before:</strong> "I lived for myself and my heart was restless."</p>\par
              <p><strong>The Turn:</strong> "During a church service, I accepted Jesus. At that moment, I felt peace."</p>\par
              <p><strong>The Result:</strong> "My anger has reduced, and I now have a deep desire to read the Word daily."</p>\par
              <p><strong>Commitment:</strong> "I commit to follow Christ and grow in His Word every day."</p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-gray-900 text-white p-6 rounded-xl mt-8">\par
              <p className="text-center font-bold text-xl mb-3">FINAL WORDS</p>\par
              <p className="text-center text-sm opacity-90 mb-4">\par
                  Your testimony is a tool for evangelism and evidence of God\rquote s work. \par
                  Sharing it during your baptismal service encourages others and glorifies God.\par
              </p>\par
              <div className="flex justify-center space-x-4 text-xs font-semibold text-blue-300">\par
                  <span>STRUCTURED</span>\par
                  <span>\bullet </span>\par
                  <span>AUTHENTIC</span>\par
                  <span>\bullet </span>\par
                  <span>CONCISE</span>\par
              </div>\par
              <div className="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-700 text-center">\par
                  <p className="italic text-xs text-gray-400">"Your story is the bridge between God's grace and someone else's need."</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\},\par
\par
\{\par
  id: 20,\par
  title: 'LESSON 20: FAQ & DOCTRINAL CLARITY',\par
  description: 'Addressing common questions, denominational differences, and providing biblical clarity on the "how" and "why" of baptism.',\par
  content: (\par
      <div className="space-y-4">\par
          <p>\par
              This final lesson ensures that new believers understand the biblical basis for baptism, navigate denominational differences with grace, and can defend their faith without confusion.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>I. INFANT BAPTISM VS. BELIEVER\rquote S BAPTISM</strong></p>\par
          <div className="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg border">\par
              <p className="text-sm mb-2"><strong>Believer\rquote s Baptism:</strong> Based on personal faith and repentance (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38; 8:36\endash 38" />). The individual must be old enough to exercise personal faith.</p>\par
              <p className="text-sm"><strong>Infant Baptism:</strong> Defended by some traditions as covenantal inclusion (<ScriptureText text="Acts 16:15; 1 Corinthians 7:14" />). However, for a public declaration of <em>personal</em> obedience, baptism must follow a personal decision to follow Christ.</p>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <p><strong>II. RE-BAPTISM: WHEN IS IT NECESSARY?</strong></p>\par
          <p className="text-sm">\par
              Baptism is a once-for-all declaration (<ScriptureText text="Romans 6:3\endash 4" />). Re-baptism is generally only necessary if the first act was <strong>invalid</strong> (e.g., occurred before true faith, or used a non-biblical formula). \par
              <em>Backsliding does not require re-baptism;</em> restoration requires repentance and renewed commitment.\par
          </p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>III. DENOMINATIONAL DIFFERENCES</strong></p>\par
          <div className="overflow-x-auto">\par
              <table className="min-w-full text-xs border">\par
                  <thead>\par
                      <tr className="bg-gray-100">\par
                          <th className="border p-2">Tradition</th>\par
                          <th className="border p-2">Practice</th>\par
                          <th className="border p-2">Primary Emphasis</th>\par
                      </tr>\par
                  </thead>\par
                  <tbody>\par
                      <tr>\par
                          <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Catholic/Orthodox</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Infant, Sprinkling/Immersion</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Sacramental grace conferred</td>\par
                      </tr>\par
                      <tr>\par
                          <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Pentecostal/Baptist</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Believer, Immersion</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Public declaration & obedience</td>\par
                      </tr>\par
                      <tr>\par
                          <td className="border p-2 font-bold">Evangelical</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Believer, Immersion</td>\par
                          <td className="border p-2">Symbol of faith & repentance</td>\par
                      </tr>\par
                  </tbody>\par
              </table>\par
          </div>\par
          <p className="text-xs italic mt-2">Note: <ScriptureText text="Romans 14:1\endash 4" /> reminds us that differences in practice should not divide the Body of Christ.</p>\par
\par
          <hr className="my-4" />\par
\par
          <p><strong>IV. CATEGORIZED FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS</strong></p>\par
          <div className="space-y-4">\par
              <details className="group border rounded-md p-2">\par
                  <summary className="font-bold text-sm cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center">\par
                      <span>Salvation & Necessity</span>\par
                      <span className="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">\f5\u9660?\f0 </span>\par
                  </summary>\par
                  <div className="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-700 space-y-2">\par
                      <p><strong>Is baptism necessary for salvation?</strong> No, salvation is by grace through faith (<ScriptureText text="Ephesians 2:8-9" />), but baptism is the first act of obedience for a believer.</p>\par
                      <p><strong>Can baptism remove sins?</strong> No, only the blood of Jesus cleanses sin. Baptism is the <em>symbol</em> of that washing.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </details>\par
\par
              <details className="group border rounded-md p-2">\par
                  <summary className="font-bold text-sm cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center">\par
                      <span>The Mode & Method</span>\par
                      <span className="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">\f5\u9660?\f0 </span>\par
                  </summary>\par
                  <div className="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-700 space-y-2">\par
                      <p><strong>Why immersion?</strong> The Greek word <em>baptizo</em> means to dip or submerge, perfectly depicting burial and resurrection.</p>\par
                      <p><strong>Jesus Only vs. Trinity?</strong> Most use the Trinity formula (<ScriptureText text="Matthew 28:19" />), while some emphasize "Jesus' Name" (<ScriptureText text="Acts 2:38" />). Both center on the authority of Christ.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </details>\par
\par
              <details className="group border rounded-md p-2">\par
                  <summary className="font-bold text-sm cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center">\par
                      <span>Personal Readiness</span>\par
                      <span className="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">\f5\u9660?\f0 </span>\par
                  </summary>\par
                  <div className="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-700 space-y-2">\par
                      <p><strong>What if I don't understand all doctrine?</strong> You don't need a theology degree; you only need to understand the Gospel and commit to Christ.</p>\par
                      <p><strong>What if I still struggle with sin?</strong> Baptism is for sinners who have found a Savior, not for people who have reached perfection.</p>\par
                  </div>\par
              </details>\par
          </div>\par
\par
          <div className="bg-blue-900 text-white p-6 rounded-xl mt-8">\par
              <p className="text-center font-bold text-xl mb-3">CONGRATULATIONS</p>\par
              <p className="text-center text-sm opacity-90 mb-4">\par
                  You have completed the full Baptismal Preparation Curriculum. You are now equipped with the knowledge, conviction, and peace to move forward in this holy act of obedience.\par
              </p>\par
              <div className="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-blue-700 text-center">\par
                  <p className="italic text-xs text-blue-300">"Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit."</p>\par
              </div>\par
          </div>\par
      </div>\par
  )\par
\}\par
];\par
\par
